WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.984 line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:11.765 --> 00:00:13.140 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
So thank you very

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.270 line:84%
much for sharing so
generously with our esteemed,

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.820 line:90%
distinguished, dear guest.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.940 line:84%
It was very-- I learnt a lot
through the back and forth

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.730 line:84%
between you and Professor
Casimir's questions

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:27.450 line:90%
and comments.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.870 line:84%
I Thank you for sharing
so much knowledge.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.455 line:90%
It's just amazing.

00:00:32.455 --> 00:00:34.080 line:84%
And I'm so glad--
thank you, Tom, thank

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.140 line:84%
you, MIT OpenCourseWare,
for recording this.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:37.960 line:90%
This is historical.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.450 line:90%
This is historical.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.700 line:84%
And it does have
the potential, I

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.990 line:84%
think, to reach out to folks
on both ends of the spectrum,

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.820 line:84%
both the "Lakou" end and
the "Lagrandyab" end,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.850 line:84%
both the colonized and the
colonizer, in many ways,

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.870 line:84%
in a way that can
enrich all of us.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:57.370 line:90%
So thank you for that.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:02.430 line:84%
So now we're going to hear
Ti Jan Bosal introduce

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.940 line:84%
this beautiful chapter, a
powerful chapter on the power

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.710 line:84%
and beauty of the
sovereign people.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.950 line:84%
And then we go around the
room and ask questions.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:10.950 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Thank you.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:11.910 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: And discuss.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.827 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: And I'm
really happy to be here,

00:01:13.827 --> 00:01:18.800 line:84%
because I'm in a
real context where

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.950 line:84%
you feel at home, because
everything you have said up

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:25.780 line:84%
till now, even from
the very beginning,

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.290 line:84%
gives you the sense what
is "decolonial reading,"

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:32.770 line:84%
"decolonial study," because
it's something that starts

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.120 line:90%
with your personal position.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.650 line:90%
You must know where you are.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.700 line:84%
You must be aware
of where you are.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:41.230 line:84%
And from that, you see the
world, you know what I mean?

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:48.370 line:84%
Because in fact, this is what
is your power, the possibility

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.550 line:84%
of seeing the world from your
position, not from the position

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:52.630 line:90%
they have given you.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.520 line:90%
You find your position.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.630 line:90%
You define your position.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.380 line:84%
And from your position
you are seeing the world.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.350 line:84%
And something I have written
and in my mind is fundamental,

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:08.470 line:84%
the use of social sciences,
the raison d'être of social

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.020 line:90%
sciences is to regain--

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:13.390 line:84%
to give to those who
have been dispossessed,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.100 line:90%
to re-empower them.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.750 line:84%
If not, I don't know
why they study--

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.290 line:84%
I am not there to empower
those who are empowered.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.210 line:90%
I have been disempowered.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:30.580 line:84%
And my aim is to study,
to work so I can regain

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.070 line:84%
my position over
myself, over my people,

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.200 line:84%
and for my people
to move forward.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.500 line:84%
Now, this implies
understanding myself,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.110 line:84%
understanding my people,
and understanding the other.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.970 line:90%
And then I move from there.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:49.930 line:84%
So the beauty and power
of the sovereign people

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:56.860 line:84%
is that we come from
a set of individuals,

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.880 line:84%
because capitalism and European
capitalism, modern time

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.320 line:84%
capitalism is based
on the desegregation

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.730 line:90%
of the communities.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.320 line:90%
You take the communities.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.380 line:84%
You make of them
naked individuals.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.630 line:84%
And then you give
them the possibility

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:18.050 line:84%
of rejoining within
your own norms--

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:19.790 line:84%
now called the
"syndicate," called

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:21.620 line:84%
the trade union,
whatever you want,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:26.520 line:84%
but after you make of
them naked individuals.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:32.480 line:84%
We are taken from Africa, in the
case of Saint-Domingue, Haiti,

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.270 line:90%
at least 24 different nations.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.830 line:90%
Now they mix them all.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.920 line:90%
They mix us up in the country.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:43.220 line:84%
And we are going to
recreate ourselves.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:45.900 line:90%
How do we do that?

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.740 line:84%
First of all, we
create with nothing,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.040 line:84%
eh, a group, a social
group just to protect us,

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.840 line:84%
because when you reach there
as an enslaved person, now

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.070 line:84%
they are going to beat you,
to-- well, to destroy you, no?

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.570 line:84%
When you finish working, the
few times they give you to rest,

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.640 line:90%
you will find consolation.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:09.840 line:90%
You will find affection.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:10.740 line:90%
You will find love.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.900 line:90%
You will find reciprocity.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.430 line:90%
You will find solidarity.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:15.780 line:90%
You give and take.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.209 line:90%
You will find human relations.

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:21.779 line:84%
So you will create, you
recreate a social group.

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:25.110 line:84%
I mean, this social
group you create

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:29.250 line:84%
is going to have a set of
principles, a set of norms, no?

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:34.560 line:84%
And these norms are going to
give way to new individuals.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.320 line:84%
You're going to create a
new individual according

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.080 line:84%
to your own norms, to
your own principles.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:45.240 line:84%
And these new
individuals will be power

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.100 line:90%
in front of the other power.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.290 line:84%
So that gives you
the possibility

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:52.110 line:90%
of discovering out of nothing.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.960 line:84%
Now you're going to
imagine another power.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:01.940 line:84%
And it is so important that
the one who brought you

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.640 line:84%
in the Caribbean or in
the plantation system

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.880 line:84%
to exploit you is
willing to spend

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:11.390 line:84%
all his efforts to destroy
you because you are something.

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.870 line:84%
And you're going to be
aware of that something.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:19.240 line:84%
For instance, they will
say Vodou or whatever we do

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.230 line:84%
or be whatever it
is is superstition.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.820 line:84%
If it is superstition,
it is useless.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:24.500 line:90%
Let me use it.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.500 line:90%
It's not doing anything.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:26.700 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.610 line:84%
If it has no solution, why
do you make so much effort

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.140 line:90%
to destroy it?

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.080 line:84%
I mean-- so therefore you
are acknowledging that, no?

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:37.360 line:84%
Another element that
is important also--

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:41.900 line:84%
granted, you want me to do that,
I don't do it, you beat me up.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.890 line:84%
You can even kill
me so I can do it.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:47.080 line:84%
But I never see you
killing a donkey

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.440 line:84%
because it doesn't
want to drink water,

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.320 line:84%
because you know the
donkey is an animal.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.300 line:90%
I mean, so they know--

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.760 line:84%
but me you're going to
beat me until I'm dead.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:01.720 line:84%
Therefore, you acknowledge
that I'm equal to you.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.090 line:84%
If I was not equal
to you, you would not

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:05.110 line:90%
beat me until I am dead.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:06.318 line:90%
You would do like the donkey.

00:06:06.318 --> 00:06:07.970 line:90%
I'd let you beat me [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.630 line:84%
No, you beat me, because
you know I have a will.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.660 line:90%
And this will escapes to you--

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:14.500 line:90%
escapes you.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:16.270 line:90%
And this is my power.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:19.420 line:84%
OK, with that power in
front of you, might--

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.940 line:84%
because you really
have a might--

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:27.790 line:84%
I am going now to
create myself, a being,

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:30.580 line:90%
my sovereignty with nothing.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.320 line:84%
I'm going to recreate
a new society.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:33.680 line:90%
That is the beauty of it.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.550 line:84%
And this is what we
have to be aware of.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.910 line:84%
Now, it is not
that I feel strong.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:46.040 line:84%
No, no, no, I'm going to create
a whole set of institutions.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:46.880 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:53.225 line:84%
A language, a
religion, villages,

00:06:53.225 --> 00:06:58.460 line:84%
a set of institutions that I
create to take care of myself

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:03.350 line:84%
and to reproduce myself and
also to have accountability

00:07:03.350 --> 00:07:05.967 line:90%
for the members of the group.

00:07:05.967 --> 00:07:06.800 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.090 line:84%
So I create another universe
in front of your universe, no?

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:16.743 line:84%
And your universe, since you
despise me, cannot see me.

00:07:16.743 --> 00:07:18.410 line:84%
I mean, even when
they see me, they say,

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.437 line:84%
"Oh, these are stupid
things niggers are doing,"

00:07:20.437 --> 00:07:21.270 line:90%
you see what I mean?

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.420 line:84%
Even when they see,
they cannot adjust.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:26.810 line:84%
So therefore,
myself, to survive,

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:29.840 line:84%
I first learn what
you have in mind.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.270 line:90%
And I, in group--

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.970 line:84%
no, I'm going to
imagine how to bypass

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.090 line:84%
what you have in mind to
the small possibility.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:38.900 line:90%
Sometimes I can't.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:39.710 line:90%
Sometime I can.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:42.540 line:84%
But I will never lose the
opportunity of doing that.

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:47.480 line:84%
This is-- therefore, some
people coming down here

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.120 line:84%
or brought down here
against their will

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.630 line:84%
after much suffering,
because you do not

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:57.320 line:84%
leave Africa as a tourist,
no, you are beaten.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:01.290 line:84%
And you have to walk sometime
hundreds and hundreds of miles.

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:03.540 line:84%
They put you in circumstances
you have never imagined.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.498 line:84%
They put you on the boat,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:08:05.498 --> 00:08:06.110 line:90%
You come here.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:09.860 line:84%
And when you come here, now
they start humiliating you,

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.370 line:84%
doing things that are
beyond imagination, no?

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:14.150 line:90%
They sell you.

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:15.770 line:84%
The stamp you, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.460 line:84%
I will not describe
all the things.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:25.220 line:84%
But in that, I would
say, infinite poverty

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:29.240 line:84%
and destruction,
you're going to reborn.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.330 line:84%
This is the beauty of
the sovereign people.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.370 line:90%
Now, how do you do that?

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:36.630 line:90%
I say you create a community.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:41.159 line:84%
But the community you created
in the plantation itself--

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:45.030 line:84%
I mean, while you're
working on the plantation,

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:47.880 line:84%
you're creating social
groups, in spite

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.100 line:84%
of the intention of the
person to call you Black.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:52.080 line:90%
"Black" is a colonial world.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:53.490 line:90%
In Africa, you're not Black.

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:54.570 line:90%
You can be Ashanti.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:55.470 line:90%
You can be Negro.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:56.400 line:90%
You can be Igbo.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.980 line:84%
You can be anything but Black,
because everybody is Black.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.740 line:84%
So Black does not
distinguish one to another.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:07.110 line:84%
I mean, so you come here,
and you create a little group

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.850 line:90%
on the plantation.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:12.360 line:84%
Now in that group, there
are others who really cannot

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:14.190 line:90%
survive in the plantation.

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:15.180 line:90%
They will run away.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:18.810 line:84%
And you'll have a set
of bandits and people

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:23.460 line:84%
stole, living out of stealing
goods, coming in and out,

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:25.350 line:84%
looking for weapons,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:29.160 line:84%
They are set of generally
male maroons, no?

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:32.970 line:84%
Those male maroons and
you are in contact.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:36.000 line:84%
And at some point in time,
they will with their ladies

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:38.670 line:84%
go and create the
free village system.

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:39.990 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:43.410 line:84%
Now, from the people
on the plantation,

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:46.410 line:84%
the independent
fighters or brigands

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.350 line:84%
or whatever they call them
to the village system,

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:53.790 line:84%
you have a constant movement
of ideas [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:58.280 line:84%
And this is done through the
language, through the language.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:01.300 line:90%
So you have now a whole--

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.240 line:84%
in the plantation system
before independence,

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:10.240 line:84%
you have a whole movement
of concept, of habits,

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:14.110 line:84%
of conducts that teach you
the possibility of bypassing

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:17.320 line:84%
the system and defending
yourself against the system.

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:20.950 line:84%
This is possible, particularly
in the larger Antilles

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:25.510 line:84%
or in Suriname or
in the Guyanas, no?

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:32.240 line:84%
And this gives what I call the
"counter-plantation system,"

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:37.870 line:84%
which is the basis
for the nation.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.480 line:84%
Not yet, the nation
is being born,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.510 line:84%
but I mean the Haitians
or the Jamaicans,

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:44.440 line:90%
et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:48.730 line:84%
This is one line
of the development.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:51.310 line:84%
In the case for instance of
the smaller island like Saint

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:53.860 line:84%
Vincent, Barbados,
Martinique, they

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:57.350 line:84%
have to negotiate
within the system.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:58.960 line:84%
Take Barbados as
the best example,

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:04.360 line:84%
because they were the
seat of the Royal Navy.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:08.470 line:84%
So you better behave yourself
or they kill you, that's all.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.920 line:84%
I mean, so you have to learn to
negotiate within their system.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:17.050 line:84%
But this is the
same basic position.

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:20.110 line:84%
They are different, because
the condition in which they

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:21.700 line:84%
are negotiating are
not the condition

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:25.310 line:84%
in which, for instance,
Surinamese is negotiating.

00:11:25.310 --> 00:11:27.100 line:84%
And the Surinamese
have the Amerindians

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:31.900 line:84%
who will show them how the
Amazonian et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:35.780 line:84%
In any event, this
is what we are doing.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.170 line:84%
We prepare ourselves
in the negotiation

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:45.010 line:84%
to create the group of people
who can see another reality,

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:49.540 line:84%
create another universe, and a
universe that the "powerful,"

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:52.990 line:90%
so-called, tend to erase, no?

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:55.900 line:84%
And we confront
those two universes.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:12:04.300 line:84%
Now when the imperial colonial
countries enter in conflicts,

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:07.510 line:84%
now they create a possibility
for you to break in,

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:08.380 line:90%
you see what I mean?

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:10.390 line:84%
So there is no difference
between the Haitian

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:13.830 line:84%
and the Jamaican except what I
told you what happened outside.

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:15.580 line:84%
There is no difference
between the Haitian

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:18.610 line:84%
or Jamaican and the
Barbadian, except that one is

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:19.960 line:90%
in the small island, which is--

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:22.600 line:84%
Barbados is something
like 300 times

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:25.360 line:84%
smaller than the whole
island there in Haiti,

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:28.090 line:84%
because we have an island in
front of Port-au-Prince, La

00:12:28.090 --> 00:12:34.000 line:84%
Gonave, which is twice bigger
than Barbados, an island only.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.550 line:84%
The country is something 70
times, if you put the D.R.--

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.450 line:84%
So you can imagine the
possibility of negotiation

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:43.330 line:84%
in this island and
of the Barbadians

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:44.980 line:90%
are quite different, no?

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:46.750 line:84%
Not to speak of the
condition in which

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:50.410 line:90%
Barbados was settled, no--

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:53.440 line:84%
Barbados was settled
by couples, by British

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:57.220 line:84%
who came with their
wives, so that one thing,

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:00.280 line:84%
they do not have a lot
of people of color.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:01.612 line:90%
They use-- no, no.

00:13:01.612 --> 00:13:04.240 line:84%
They have, but they
do not have a lot.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:07.670 line:84%
That's one condition
that we are all,

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:16.000 line:84%
in fact, the basic set of
position of self-respect,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.490 line:84%
sovereignty, group autonomy--
this is what is our base.

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:28.180 line:84%
So beauty-- the beauty is
that in the worse condition

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:31.990 line:84%
of living, thing we could
not possibly imagine that is

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:32.590 line:90%
happening--

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:34.630 line:84%
I do not want to
enter in the relation

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:38.200 line:84%
between the white males
and their enslaved

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.950 line:84%
and their kids and
their daughters,

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:45.010 line:84%
what they have-- no, we
can just imagine that they

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:46.645 line:90%
think they are all-powerful.

00:13:46.645 --> 00:13:47.770 line:90%
They do whatever they want.

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:50.530 line:84%
They destroy themselves,
but that's another problem.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:52.580 line:90%
But forget about that.

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:56.800 line:84%
We simply create our
world out of nothing.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:59.620 line:84%
And it's the
Caribbean world, no?

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:06.130 line:84%
When I say out of nothing,
we do have resources

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:07.550 line:90%
we remember from Africa.

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:11.980 line:84%
But they are individuals
who remember that.

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:16.720 line:84%
We have an advantage though over
the First Nations in America.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:19.810 line:84%
The First Nations--
all the Jews, also--

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:23.320 line:84%
they are people
that were exploited.

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:28.030 line:84%
But they were a nation
that were exploited.

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:32.620 line:84%
They have been forced to
witness the destruction

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:34.170 line:90%
of their nation.

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:37.200 line:84%
You see the Mayas, the
Aztec, or the Incas--

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:38.790 line:84%
they could see the
Spaniards coming.

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:40.650 line:90%
And they have lived that.

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:42.930 line:84%
In our case, no, for
instance, assuming

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:46.410 line:84%
that they get into Yoruba
country or Igbo country,

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:49.770 line:84%
they beat them, and they take
you, and they bring you here.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:52.600 line:90%
You would say, oh OK, I lost.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:54.420 line:90%
I had just bad luck.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:55.290 line:90%
They got me.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.840 line:84%
But the country, nobody
can beat the Igbos.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:01.500 line:84%
Even if it is not true, but you
will imagine that you have not

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.110 line:84%
witnessed your society
being humiliated,

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:07.840 line:90%
your kings being a--

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:09.570 line:84%
yes, humiliated, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.010 line:90%
So we have at least that.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:14.670 line:84%
We start with local
experience from the day

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:17.400 line:90%
we came here in America.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:20.170 line:84%
And from that local
events, we move forward

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:25.920 line:84%
so we can create a new
society, a new world

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:29.100 line:90%
with our experience, not with--

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:34.590 line:84%
we do not carry all the
tradition of our society.

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:39.530 line:84%
And we do not carry the
"deuil," the sorrow.

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:42.680 line:84%
Or for instance, there
are some wonderful scene

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.870 line:84%
in the-- among
the first nations.

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:46.730 line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] going
all over Bolivia,

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:50.840 line:84%
where they are
playing the conquest.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.157 line:84%
I mean, the Amerindians
are playing the conquest.

00:15:53.157 --> 00:15:53.990 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:15:53.990 --> 00:16:00.080 line:84%
You can see all the sadness
in the winning of the alien

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:02.840 line:84%
over-- we don't have
that possibility.

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:07.670 line:84%
And in general, our
musical production

00:16:07.670 --> 00:16:09.170 line:90%
is quite different from theirs.

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:13.340 line:84%
So this is what
I call the beauty

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:20.630 line:84%
of our nation, what
we have witnessed,

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:21.650 line:90%
what we have created.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:25.310 line:84%
The problem is that we have to
make our people aware of that.

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:28.210 line:84%
And this is what
the system and what

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:34.730 line:84%
Professor DeGraff was showing,
wants you not to see, you see?

00:16:34.730 --> 00:16:36.980 line:84%
For instance, the
beauty of Kreyòl--

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:42.680 line:84%
Kreyòl is switching the
meaning of key function.

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:47.060 line:84%
One of my great friends and
Professor Guillermo Bonfil

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:51.740 line:84%
from Mexico, he said, "Indian,"
"Negroes" are colonial concept.

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:55.700 line:84%
There were no Indians in America
before Christopher Columbus

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:57.500 line:90%
made his mistakes.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:16:59.630 line:90%
Negroes, that you call Negroes--

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.130 line:84%
I mean, I don't
know if I'm a Negro.

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:03.890 line:84%
I mean, that's meaningless
that I am a Negro,

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:05.210 line:90%
et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:08.750 line:84%
Those are key concepts
of colonialism.

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:09.785 line:90%
They create race.

00:17:09.785 --> 00:17:13.579 line:84%
A set of languages in
Africa has no word for race.

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:16.579 line:84%
And in Haiti, "race"
means "family"

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:21.740 line:84%
according to Old French, as
in "the race of DeGraff,"

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:23.150 line:90%
"the race of Casimir."

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.220 line:84%
They say, "Watch out with
the race of Rodriguez.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:27.290 line:84%
They are really bad
people, all of them.

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:29.390 line:90%
They are witches."

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:34.870 line:84%
But they mean the family, not
the race what the West covers.

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:36.380 line:90%
So we came.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.300 line:90%
And we create another concept.

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:41.630 line:84%
And I don't know if you know,
but as everybody knows and I

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:44.300 line:84%
give the example so
many times, "whites"

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:47.810 line:84%
do not mean what it means
in French or in English.

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:53.410 line:84%
Whites-- for instance, I have
a very good friend of mine from

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:54.460 line:90%
Jamaica I say--

00:17:54.460 --> 00:17:56.200 line:84%
when I do something
in my company,

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:57.850 line:90%
I say I trick the white man.

00:17:57.850 --> 00:18:00.980 line:84%
But I don't mean white
men, the man who is white.

00:18:00.980 --> 00:18:04.000 line:84%
I mean, I trick the
technology, the dominant one,

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:08.060 line:84%
the one who want the
algorithm to work that way.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:09.433 line:90%
I make it work another way.

00:18:09.433 --> 00:18:11.350 line:84%
I mean, this is why I
call it "the white man."

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:15.730 line:84%
And in fact, in my proposition,
"white" means in Haiti

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:19.630 line:84%
the responsible of the primitive
accumulation of capital.

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:21.850 line:84%
We need to be the dominant
capitalist fellow, who

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:24.760 line:84%
comes in, takes whatever he
sees, that he is and he take it

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:26.140 line:90%
if he wants it, period.

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:28.360 line:90%
No, he has been-- that's why.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:31.210 line:84%
So for instance, if he
comes home they will say,

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:32.662 line:84%
"there is a Negro
asking for you."

00:18:32.662 --> 00:18:34.120 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Yeah, that's right.

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:34.370 line:90%
That's right.

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:34.830 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Period.

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:36.910 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So if Tom goes
to Haiti, Tom becomes a Negro.

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:37.300 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: A Negro.

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:39.758 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Because "Negro"
means human being in Haiti.

00:18:39.758 --> 00:18:43.270 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Period, and
the reverse is true, too.

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:45.910 line:84%
My daughter is like
you all, my son, also.

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:49.300 line:84%
And they are all "white"
because they are strangers.

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:50.740 line:90%
They can't speak Kreyòl.

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:53.350 line:90%
They don't know the habits.

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:54.310 line:90%
They are too--

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:56.680 line:90%
I mean, it's not discriminatory.

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.440 line:84%
It's just simply they do
not know what's going on.

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:02.560 line:84%
They do not know the
rules of the game.

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:03.380 line:90%
That's it.

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:08.560 line:84%
So you have changed the key
concept of the dominant system.

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:12.773 line:84%
And you have create your world
where "tout moun se moun,"

00:19:12.773 --> 00:19:13.690 line:90%
everybody is a person.

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:16.420 line:90%
You have create the word "moun."

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:17.920 line:84%
I mean, the word
"moun" has nothing

00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:20.830 line:84%
to do with white,
black, male, female.

00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:21.940 line:90%
It just mean person.

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:26.200 line:84%
Every person is a person, and
should be respected as such.

00:19:26.200 --> 00:19:29.912 line:84%
This is something you have
done when you are in the "très

00:19:29.912 --> 00:19:34.210 line:90%
fond," in the depth of misery.

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:37.840 line:84%
To get out and to
create that group that

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:41.290 line:84%
can survive oppression,
you had to erase that.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:43.810 line:84%
This is why the
Igbo disappeared,

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:46.600 line:84%
the Congo disappeared,
the Hausa disappeared.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:48.430 line:90%
You become a moun.

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:51.880 line:84%
And you have to be-- and
as far as I know in Haiti,

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:55.390 line:84%
the worst of all sin
is to destroy life.

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:57.310 line:84%
You have to respect
life, period.

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:59.830 line:90%
You're not cut tree for nothing.

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:03.430 line:84%
You don't kill an
insect for nothing.

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:05.710 line:90%
No, no, no, no, that is a sin.

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:07.270 line:90%
This is what is a sin.

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:12.100 line:84%
So the beauty of
the sovereign people

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:17.020 line:84%
is that it has been able
to invent its sovereignty

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:19.870 line:84%
and invent itself
as distinct from.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:21.850 line:84%
And this is where
also it has been

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:27.040 line:84%
able to show you, make you
see what power means in fact.

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.350 line:84%
Power doesn't mean
that you're stronger,

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:30.880 line:90%
you have more strength.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:32.560 line:84%
No, no, that is
not really power.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:36.260 line:84%
This is appearance of power
because your strengths,

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:38.950 line:84%
you have it because
I'm powerful.

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:42.270 line:84%
You see, I like to
tell my friend--

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:44.020 line:84%
well, in fact, that's
an idea that is in--

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:46.810 line:90%
Eric Williams developed, no?

00:20:46.810 --> 00:20:49.900 line:84%
Let's see, the Haitians--
to beat the Haitians,

00:20:49.900 --> 00:20:52.510 line:90%
Napoleon sent 40,000 persons.

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:53.650 line:90%
And they lost.

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:56.620 line:84%
So we are stronger
40,000 plus 1,

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:59.530 line:84%
at least [LAUGHS]
because they lost.

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:02.020 line:84%
So if you want to
know what power is,

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:03.580 line:90%
ask us, because we beat him.

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:05.410 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:07.300 line:84%
You must understand
what power is.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:12.430 line:84%
Obviously, in Haiti, they are
destroying the use of Kreyòl.

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:15.370 line:84%
They do not want Kreyòl,
as you have seen,

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:18.420 line:90%
because Kreyòl is powerful.

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:23.220 line:84%
You must be aware of this power
that you control, you manage.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:24.870 line:84%
And you are the
one who invented.

00:21:24.870 --> 00:21:28.860 line:84%
When I say something in French,
the French Academy will decide

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:31.500 line:84%
or in Spanish,
the Royal Academy,

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:34.470 line:84%
the Académie Royale will
decide if it is correct,

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:37.080 line:84%
if you can say it or
if you cannot say it.

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.900 line:84%
If I'm talking Jamaican,
that the shanty town

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.060 line:84%
that will decide if
this can be said or not.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:44.130 line:84%
It's not the government,
not even the government.

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:51.150 line:84%
It is us, us in our most inner
existence without authority.

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:56.880 line:84%
It is the agreement between us
that we decide that what is,

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:02.280 line:84%
for instance, shack up, shacking
up, if this is acceptable

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:02.790 line:90%
or not.

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:04.410 line:90%
It is not an authority.

00:22:04.410 --> 00:22:07.020 line:84%
It is the consensus
between the few people.

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:08.670 line:90%
This is what sovereignty is.

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:11.380 line:90%
So I will stop there.

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:12.107 line:90%
[CLAPPING]

00:22:12.107 --> 00:22:12.690 line:90%
Oh boy, merci.

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:15.990 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:18.540 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: What you
said about white reminds

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:20.070 line:90%
me of James Baldwin.

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:21.010 line:90%
He has a statement--

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:22.420 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Oh yeah, exactly.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:24.870 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: --that "white"
is a metaphor for power.

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:26.820 line:84%
There's no such
thing as whiteness.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:28.233 line:90%
"White" is Chase Manhattan Bank.

00:22:28.233 --> 00:22:29.400 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yeah, exactly.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:31.260 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Yeah,
yeah, so thank you

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:34.980 line:84%
for this amazing summary
of your entire approach

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:37.420 line:90%
really to Haitian history.

00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:39.840 line:90%
So all right, so--

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:42.810 line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's a lot
like the white thing--

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:46.770 line:90%
for me, I hear it as--

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:51.690 line:84%
it's a political position
sort of of your relationality

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:57.630 line:84%
to the world, to things, to
land, to food, to people,

00:22:57.630 --> 00:22:59.427 line:84%
rather than the
color of your skin--

00:22:59.427 --> 00:23:00.510 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yes, indeed.

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:04.708 line:84%
AUDIENCE: --your mental state
of how you see the world.

00:23:04.708 --> 00:23:06.750 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: OK, so
you can go around the room

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:07.830 line:90%
and ask questions.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:10.440 line:84%
[INAUDIBLE],, actually Gaby
had her hand up very quickly.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:11.025 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:11.025 --> 00:23:12.150 line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] I think I can--

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:13.703 line:90%
Merelin, you-- OK, all right.

00:23:13.703 --> 00:23:15.120 line:84%
STUDENT: What was
interesting that

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:18.750 line:84%
stood out to me in the reading
was cultural homogenization

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:19.410 line:90%
and how--

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:20.160 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Come again.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:21.690 line:84%
STUDENT: Cultural
homogenization--

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:23.190 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR:
Cultural imagination?

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:25.620 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Homogenization, homogenization,

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.020 line:90%
cultural homogenization.

00:23:28.020 --> 00:23:31.650 line:84%
STUDENT: --of how customs,
habits, and aspirations were

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:35.820 line:84%
not pursued by the racist and
Eurocentric public authorities

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:40.410 line:84%
but rather productions
carried out by the sufferers,

00:23:40.410 --> 00:23:41.970 line:90%
by the sufferers.

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:45.150 line:84%
And I thought that it was
super interesting to think

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:47.820 line:84%
of it that way, because I'd
personally always thought

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:50.190 line:90%
of like, oh, they classify us--

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.580 line:84%
like, we're classified
as Black, because there's

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:58.020 line:84%
a racist system that sees us
all as like just one person

00:23:58.020 --> 00:23:59.760 line:84%
and not like
different ambitions.

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:03.180 line:84%
But I never thought of it
as like a positive light

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:04.500 line:90%
like how you spun it.

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:09.450 line:84%
How it wasn't-- it was an
aspect of our survival.

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:14.880 line:84%
We had to unify under
this one form of culture.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:17.130 line:84%
And I actually thought
that was interesting.

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:20.370 line:84%
And I was wondering,
how did you get

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:25.290 line:84%
to that point of realizing
that we formed this one

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:29.400 line:90%
homogenized culture not out of--

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:32.610 line:84%
just simply out of
spite, in some cases,

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.980 line:84%
out of survival
and out of spite.

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:36.480 line:90%
How did you get to this point?

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:37.140 line:90%
That's what I--

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:38.432 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: I see your point.

00:24:38.432 --> 00:24:42.330 line:84%
To be-- I think
now you make me see

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:46.920 line:84%
that, I think I should go back
to my friend I just mentioned,

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:48.090 line:90%
Guillermo Bonfil.

00:24:48.090 --> 00:24:53.310 line:90%
Guillermo is an anthropologist.

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:58.100 line:84%
He said, "Indian is an
invention of the Spaniards."

00:24:58.100 --> 00:25:00.960 line:90%
When you take an Inuit--

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:02.520 line:84%
you know Inuit,
no, from Eskimo--

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:08.160 line:84%
and you take a Tupi, or
Mapuche, or Maya, or an Inca,

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:09.700 line:84%
and you call all
of them "Indian."

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:10.200 line:90%
"Indian"?

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:11.230 line:90%
What do you mean?

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:13.560 line:84%
Why do you want
them to be Indians?

00:25:13.560 --> 00:25:18.420 line:84%
I mean, what does somebody
who live his life in snow

00:25:18.420 --> 00:25:25.020 line:84%
has to do with Tupi or
Seminole or whatever it is?

00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:27.000 line:90%
Because you want them to work.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:30.150 line:84%
And you homogenize them
in your imagination

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:32.670 line:84%
to make to work and to
exploit them the way you

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:34.950 line:90%
want to invent that lie, OK?

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.260 line:90%
So this is one thing he put.

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:40.600 line:84%
And I start saying
that therefore

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:42.250 line:90%
is a colonial concept.

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:44.440 line:90%
"White" is a colonial concept.

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:48.700 line:84%
How does the Haitian
call himself?

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:52.360 line:84%
And I found in that book of
de Vastey, our first Haitian

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:57.400 line:84%
historian, he called the book
The Colonial System Unveiled.

00:25:57.400 --> 00:25:58.600 line:90%
It is in English.

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:01.630 line:90%
It's 90 pages the book has.

00:26:01.630 --> 00:26:04.870 line:84%
And I realize in these
90 pages, de Vastey

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:08.950 line:84%
used 100 times the word
"malere yo" so "malere."

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:11.440 line:90%
And we call ourselves "malere."

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:14.500 line:84%
And "malere" can be a
rich man or poor man.

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:16.820 line:90%
It means you're fragile.

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:18.700 line:90%
You are alone.

00:26:18.700 --> 00:26:20.950 line:90%
You are vulnerable.

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:23.350 line:84%
So even if you reach
that way, "malere" now,

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:27.500 line:84%
because you can come, fell
down, and break your leg.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.710 line:84%
Not only that, the
importance of the "malere"

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:34.060 line:84%
is that I was in Africa going
to a picnic with my girlfriend.

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:36.670 line:84%
And all of a sudden came
from bandits for no reason

00:26:36.670 --> 00:26:37.300 line:90%
to pick me up.

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:41.650 line:84%
And here I am, my girlfriend
in Saint Vincent as a slave,

00:26:41.650 --> 00:26:44.350 line:90%
me in Cuba as another slave.

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:51.880 line:84%
We have done nothing to deserve
that "sort", that destiny, no?

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:53.950 line:90%
We are the "malere" no?

00:26:53.950 --> 00:26:58.210 line:84%
And up to now today, the Haitian
defined himself as "malere."

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:02.890 line:84%
And this is why my great friend
and translator, Laurent DuBois,

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:06.070 line:84%
takes from Bob Marley
the word "sufferer."

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:07.210 line:90%
We are sufferers.

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:10.330 line:84%
It is not that we are a
proletarian who came from

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:15.250 line:84%
and we could move
through social mobility

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:16.840 line:90%
outside of the proletariat.

00:27:16.840 --> 00:27:19.900 line:84%
We do not have a common
history with the bourgeoisie.

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:22.570 line:84%
The bourgeois and the
proletariat in Europe

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:24.910 line:84%
come from the "bourg,"
from the city.

00:27:24.910 --> 00:27:27.910 line:84%
And they fought
together, for instance,

00:27:27.910 --> 00:27:29.770 line:84%
against the nobility
and the clergy.

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:31.690 line:84%
And after the French
Revolution, they

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:34.010 line:84%
split when capitalism
started moving.

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:35.350 line:90%
But they have the same history.

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:36.520 line:90%
They have the same language.

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:37.687 line:90%
They have the same religion.

00:27:37.687 --> 00:27:41.840 line:84%
They have a whole hundreds
of-- no, that's not my case.

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:42.940 line:90%
I just-- Boop!

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:45.028 line:84%
They put me together in
front of that "planteur"

00:27:45.028 --> 00:27:45.820 line:90%
[plantation owner].

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:47.860 line:84%
And I'm a "malere"
because I've done nothing

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:52.700 line:84%
to deserve that suffering
thing that happened.

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:54.520 line:84%
So when I find the
word "sufferer,"

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:55.940 line:90%
I say, oh boy, that's it.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:28:00.340 line:84%
And this is the Kreyòl who
gave it to me, "malere."

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:04.600 line:84%
A Haitian do not say I'm poor
or I'm-- no, I'm "malere."

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:06.520 line:84%
And you can call
anybody "malere."

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:08.440 line:90%
"Gade figi malereèz sa a, non"--

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:10.765 line:90%
look at that poor lady.

00:28:10.765 --> 00:28:11.890 line:90%
You know what I mean?

00:28:11.890 --> 00:28:12.570 line:90%
Full stop.

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:14.080 line:90%
But that has nothing to do.

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:18.280 line:84%
OK, that means also that if
I get out of the "malheur"--

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:22.630 line:84%
of that misery, which is
not something that came from

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:25.300 line:84%
my will or from my
effort, it's an accident--

00:28:25.300 --> 00:28:26.680 line:90%
an accident put me in it.

00:28:26.680 --> 00:28:29.030 line:84%
An accident will
take me out of it.

00:28:29.030 --> 00:28:31.780 line:84%
My mother and my father
could work their life.

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:34.390 line:84%
They will not make
of me a professor.

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:37.360 line:84%
Is one day an accident, a
friend of mine was minister--

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:39.190 line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] minister of what?

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:40.360 line:90%
They found a scholarship.

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:42.550 line:90%
And I get out of it.

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:44.110 line:84%
Obviously I'm not
going to lose--

00:28:44.110 --> 00:28:47.470 line:84%
But the fact of the matter
is, you don't get out of it.

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:50.440 line:84%
You don't get into it
because you're poor.

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:54.040 line:84%
You don't get out of
it because you work.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:58.390 line:84%
Therefore, you should
respect all other "malere."

00:28:58.390 --> 00:29:02.530 line:84%
So the respect is
basic all over.

00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:07.720 line:84%
In Jamaica when I was there,
they greet you, "respect."

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:10.840 line:90%
That's the greeting, "respect."

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:13.900 line:84%
That's the greeting,
because I may be very rich,

00:29:13.900 --> 00:29:18.250 line:84%
I may be very poor, but I
earned the same respect.

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:20.500 line:84%
So this is how I
would say, I think--

00:29:20.500 --> 00:29:26.980 line:84%
I get to the word "malere,"
"sufferer," because it's just

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:27.950 line:90%
in our language.

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:32.200 line:84%
So to my mind for
social scientists,

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:35.950 line:84%
you should be aware and know
and study your language.

00:29:35.950 --> 00:29:39.700 line:84%
If you have a hypothesis, you
cannot put it in your own local

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:42.520 line:84%
language, those of us
who have two languages,

00:29:42.520 --> 00:29:43.600 line:90%
you see what I mean?

00:29:43.600 --> 00:29:45.850 line:90%
[LAUGHS] He's not working.

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:49.540 line:84%
It has to be translatable
to our language,

00:29:49.540 --> 00:29:51.130 line:90%
because we're working in--

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:53.830 line:84%
if we work directly
in our language,

00:29:53.830 --> 00:29:56.392 line:90%
obviously that come out like--

00:29:56.392 --> 00:29:58.100 line:84%
I don't know if I
answered your question.

00:29:58.100 --> 00:29:59.710 line:84%
STUDENT: No, you're
good, thank you.

00:30:03.710 --> 00:30:06.080 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So are you
going to speak to-- piggyback

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:06.530 line:90%
on the [INAUDIBLE]?

00:30:06.530 --> 00:30:06.830 line:90%
Yeah.

00:30:06.830 --> 00:30:07.780 line:84%
STUDENT: Well, I would just--
yeah, something if it's not--

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:08.822 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Continue...

00:30:08.822 --> 00:30:09.545 line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:30:09.545 --> 00:30:14.032 line:84%
No, just the concept of
being a "malla-gree."

00:30:14.032 --> 00:30:14.990 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: "Malere."

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:16.954 line:84%
STUDENT: "Malla--
malere"-- it was--

00:30:16.954 --> 00:30:18.920 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: "Malere."

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:22.460 line:90%
STUDENT: "Malere," yeah.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:27.590 line:84%
It brings me to think about
the wider idea of even

00:30:27.590 --> 00:30:35.130 line:84%
like the rational movement of
thinking that we are powerful,

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:37.010 line:84%
which is the concept
of capitalism,

00:30:37.010 --> 00:30:38.450 line:90%
that we are so powerful.

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:40.010 line:90%
You work hard.

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:40.825 line:90%
You get somewhere.

00:30:40.825 --> 00:30:41.575 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yes.

00:30:41.575 --> 00:30:44.000 line:84%
STUDENT: But most people
who work really, really hard

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:45.020 line:90%
are still--

00:30:45.020 --> 00:30:45.290 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: "Malere"?

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:45.790 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:30:45.790 --> 00:30:48.950 line:84%
STUDENT: Yeah, so
but it kind of for me

00:30:48.950 --> 00:30:53.420 line:84%
brings a question that I like
to maybe hear your thoughts on

00:30:53.420 --> 00:30:55.770 line:90%
is the idea of poverty.

00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:56.900 line:90%
What is poverty?

00:30:56.900 --> 00:31:03.500 line:84%
If poverty is still a definition
based on acquiring property,

00:31:03.500 --> 00:31:06.080 line:84%
it's even like the
relationship to--

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:08.060 line:90%
also relation to land.

00:31:08.060 --> 00:31:10.910 line:84%
If-- You're rich
if you own land,

00:31:10.910 --> 00:31:13.190 line:84%
rather than if you
have relationship

00:31:13.190 --> 00:31:17.260 line:84%
to land, and also
the idea that we come

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:23.110 line:84%
in and out of circumstances
often by influences

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:25.420 line:84%
that are outside
of our own doing.

00:31:25.420 --> 00:31:27.850 line:84%
Like, I was going on a
picnic with my girlfriend.

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:31.870 line:84%
And suddenly I am an
enslaved person in Cuba.

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:39.110 line:84%
This was not my own doing,
which is specifically

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:42.723 line:84%
antithesis of the whole
capitalist approach of--

00:31:42.723 --> 00:31:43.640 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Exactly.

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:45.720 line:84%
STUDENT: --everything
is your own doing,

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:50.030 line:84%
which doesn't humble
itself to other--

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:54.920 line:84%
I'm just bringing it
up to say, how do we

00:31:54.920 --> 00:31:57.530 line:84%
provide a different
paradigm or perspective

00:31:57.530 --> 00:32:00.470 line:84%
to look at what is
poverty and what

00:32:00.470 --> 00:32:03.590 line:84%
is the human condition,
really, in terms

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:07.490 line:84%
of the arrogance of
capitalism, which continues

00:32:07.490 --> 00:32:09.470 line:90%
to destroy our countries today.

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:14.112 line:84%
Like it is-- like Haiti today
is destroyed by neoliberal--

00:32:14.112 --> 00:32:16.320 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Being the
poorest country in the world,

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:20.720 line:84%
I think this is where
I find the importance

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:23.360 line:84%
of the decolonial studies,
because you should

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:27.240 line:90%
see capitalism from your angle.

00:32:27.240 --> 00:32:29.190 line:84%
With all due respect
for the capitalists,

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:30.843 line:90%
they are a set of liars.

00:32:30.843 --> 00:32:32.010 line:90%
STUDENT: Why'd you say that?

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:33.677 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: I'm
going to tell you why.

00:32:33.677 --> 00:32:38.100 line:84%
They declared private
property is sacred.

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:39.780 line:90%
They came here in America.

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:43.140 line:84%
They took everything
that they can see.

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:44.580 line:90%
They took it, literally.

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:47.970 line:84%
Now in Haiti, they
took Saint-Domingue.

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:50.970 line:84%
And the King of
Saint-Domingue, of Spain,

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:55.410 line:84%
will give a third of
Saint-Domingue to the French.

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:58.410 line:84%
And they declare to me
private property is sacred.

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:01.320 line:84%
Why is it-- who gave
them Saint-Domingue?

00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:03.517 line:84%
How could they take
it for themselves,

00:33:03.517 --> 00:33:04.350 line:90%
you see what I mean?

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:13.320 line:84%
Use the Seminole and this
thing presented by Peck, no?

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:16.680 line:84%
You come there, you take my
land, and you take that man

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:17.850 line:90%
and you tell him he's yours?

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:19.020 line:90%
How do you manage?

00:33:19.020 --> 00:33:21.150 line:84%
I don't know if you see
that the documentary

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:22.428 line:90%
of Raoul Peck on the--

00:33:22.428 --> 00:33:24.720 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: It's called
Exterminate all the Brutes.

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:27.450 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Exterminate
all the Brutes.

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:28.440 line:90%
The brutes come.

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:30.145 line:90%
They take the country.

00:33:30.145 --> 00:33:31.270 line:90%
They take over the country.

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:32.100 line:90%
They take all the wealth.

00:33:32.100 --> 00:33:33.870 line:84%
When they finish with the
wealth in the Caribbean,

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:35.370 line:84%
they go in Mexico,
they go in Peru.

00:33:35.370 --> 00:33:37.470 line:84%
They take everything
they had, no?

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:42.150 line:84%
And they start their capitalism,
I mean, they start developing.

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:45.450 line:84%
And they're telling me
private property is sacred,

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:48.190 line:90%
that we have to respect that.

00:33:48.190 --> 00:33:48.820 line:90%
How come?

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:52.960 line:84%
If you start lying, you
seize what is not yours.

00:33:52.960 --> 00:33:55.450 line:84%
No, but you declare that
God gave me half of--

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:58.130 line:90%
whose God, yours or mine?

00:33:58.130 --> 00:34:00.260 line:84%
What do we have
to see with that?

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:04.040 line:84%
So the question of
poverty, you impoverish me,

00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:07.370 line:84%
because you define
wealth the way you want.

00:34:07.370 --> 00:34:08.570 line:90%
You take it.

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:11.389 line:84%
And you say I have
to respect it.

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:14.150 line:84%
And you have a set of
institutions-- school,

00:34:14.150 --> 00:34:16.190 line:84%
et cetera, et cetera--
to prove to me

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:19.850 line:84%
that I deserve to be poor,
for instance, I am Black,

00:34:19.850 --> 00:34:26.550 line:84%
I'm the son of Ham, and
all sort of really, lies.

00:34:26.550 --> 00:34:31.800 line:84%
Not only that, this question
of Ham-- there is a verse.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:34.290 line:84%
I don't have it in
mind, but that is good.

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:38.639 line:84%
Well, in the Exodus, it's
said if you take a person,

00:34:38.639 --> 00:34:42.630 line:84%
you sell the person,
you deserve a death.

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:44.130 line:84%
But on the other
hand, they're going

00:34:44.130 --> 00:34:46.350 line:90%
to say the son of Ham's will--

00:34:46.350 --> 00:34:48.120 line:90%
So they are a set of liars.

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:50.040 line:84%
And I do not have
to believe that.

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:52.409 line:84%
And I do not have
to respect that.

00:34:52.409 --> 00:34:55.840 line:84%
And studies made
will show that--

00:34:55.840 --> 00:35:00.990 line:84%
I mean, I'm talking about
Madiou, another historian who

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:04.470 line:84%
was a member of the oligarchy
for the whole 19th century.

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:08.430 line:84%
He said the system is
corrupt, because corruption

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:11.530 line:90%
made the colonial system.

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:17.730 line:84%
So you cannot expect today the
authorities of Haiti not to be

00:35:17.730 --> 00:35:18.900 line:90%
corrupt.

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:22.650 line:84%
Same thing in the US, the
system is based on corruption.

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:24.900 line:84%
How could you take the
land of the Indians?

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:28.290 line:84%
You go and kill Indians,
and now you make them poor.

00:35:28.290 --> 00:35:29.130 line:90%
You create them.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:30.750 line:90%
You create their poverty.

00:35:30.750 --> 00:35:32.310 line:84%
Not only you create
the poverty, you

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:36.780 line:84%
start ruling them with English
when they were being ruled.

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:38.940 line:84%
They are-- et cetera,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:35:38.940 --> 00:35:41.160 line:90%
So I think poverty and wealth--

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:45.180 line:84%
and this is why also we must be
aware of what we call wealth.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:49.380 line:84%
Wealth is what allows us to
survive, to be, to exist.

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:51.510 line:84%
This is where imagination
comes, invention comes,

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:58.000 line:84%
all non-material
knowledge will come.

00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:03.630 line:84%
I'm not belittling technology,
because you have done something

00:36:03.630 --> 00:36:04.920 line:90%
with the ways you have taken.

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:06.720 line:90%
And I want to--

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:08.220 line:90%
I respect that, no?

00:36:08.220 --> 00:36:09.780 line:90%
But this is your interest.

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:13.440 line:84%
Now my job is with
my imagination

00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:20.790 line:84%
to try to use your technology
to foster, to empower my people.

00:36:20.790 --> 00:36:23.520 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:26.100 line:84%
I mean, this is how
more or less I see.

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:29.250 line:84%
Haiti is the poorest
country in the world,

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:34.920 line:84%
according to the
establishment's views.

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:38.760 line:84%
But as I told you, Lloyd
Best, this famous economist

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:42.810 line:84%
from Trinidad, said Haiti is
the richest country in America

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:44.220 line:90%
according to Lloyd Best.

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:48.420 line:84%
[LAUGHS] And he wrote a
long article on that--

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:49.740 line:90%
I could look for it--

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:52.945 line:84%
but to show you that
you have a voice

00:36:52.945 --> 00:36:58.770 line:84%
It's not that we like
poverty, we like--

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:00.220 line:90%
no, no, that's not the point.

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:02.850 line:84%
The point is that
you respect yourself.

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:04.020 line:90%
You like yourself.

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:05.250 line:90%
You protect yourself.

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:08.535 line:84%
And these things are
quite clear also on that.

00:37:08.535 --> 00:37:09.460 line:90%
You know what I mean?

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:11.970 line:84%
This is the-- and you
must be aware of that.

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:13.080 line:90%
This is a problem.

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:15.690 line:84%
Don't let people
divert your attention

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:18.120 line:90%
towards those material things.

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:18.997 line:90%
It is important.

00:37:18.997 --> 00:37:20.580 line:84%
I have to drink water
and clean water.

00:37:20.580 --> 00:37:23.490 line:90%
I cannot do without it, no?

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:25.650 line:90%
But it is not that that made me.

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:28.860 line:84%
As we say in Haiti,
you are not going

00:37:28.860 --> 00:37:32.580 line:84%
to have that to the
graveyard with you.

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:35.737 line:84%
You're going to go to
the graveyard naked.

00:37:35.737 --> 00:37:38.070 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Let's go
around to it with [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:38.070 --> 00:37:39.930 line:84%
Write down your
question and then--

00:37:39.930 --> 00:37:42.388 line:84%
STUDENT: And it's not-- it was
just going a [? long way. ?]

00:37:42.388 --> 00:37:44.302 line:84%
STUDENT: I have four
questions into one.

00:37:44.302 --> 00:37:45.510 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Lift your mask.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:48.060 line:84%
STUDENT: Oh, I have like
four questions into one.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:51.460 line:84%
But they all center around
the counter-plantation system.

00:37:51.460 --> 00:37:53.700 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Around the
counter-plantation system?

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:56.070 line:84%
STUDENT: Yeah, so do you think
that the fact that France

00:37:56.070 --> 00:37:58.920 line:84%
was "a master away from
home" helped cultivate

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:00.390 line:90%
the counter-plantation system?

00:38:00.390 --> 00:38:03.155 line:84%
And coming from a
Dominican person,

00:38:03.155 --> 00:38:04.780 line:84%
like someone who was
governed by Spain,

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:07.020 line:84%
do you think that is also
why Haiti was able to be

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:08.560 line:90%
successful in this system?

00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:11.490 line:84%
Meanwhile, the DR, we
still have haciendas

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.530 line:90%
and "modern plantations."

00:38:13.530 --> 00:38:16.170 line:84%
And thinking about
success, how successful

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:17.670 line:84%
was the
counter-plantation system?

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:20.370 line:84%
And are we measuring
success on the basis

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:23.700 line:84%
of do our people remember or
are our people knowledgeable,

00:38:23.700 --> 00:38:27.060 line:84%
or we're measuring on a--
have we managed to dismantle

00:38:27.060 --> 00:38:29.520 line:84%
the capitalist society that
the Pearl of the Antilles

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:30.840 line:90%
was built upon?

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:33.570 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: I'm going
to answer the first half

00:38:33.570 --> 00:38:37.290 line:84%
and then you reformulate
the second half.

00:38:37.290 --> 00:38:41.910 line:84%
The hacienda could be
the proper translation

00:38:41.910 --> 00:38:43.770 line:90%
for the word plantation.

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:47.670 line:84%
So the French never had the
counter-plantation system.

00:38:47.670 --> 00:38:50.550 line:90%
The French has an "hacienda."

00:38:50.550 --> 00:38:53.670 line:84%
It organized the
hacienda properly

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:57.330 line:84%
as a capitalist system,
a rational system where

00:38:57.330 --> 00:39:02.100 line:84%
every single input
oriented toward an output

00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:04.290 line:84%
called a merchandise
or commodity

00:39:04.290 --> 00:39:06.060 line:90%
to produce more commodities.

00:39:06.060 --> 00:39:08.550 line:84%
That is the plantation,
the hacienda.

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:12.840 line:84%
Now to save some
money, the owner

00:39:12.840 --> 00:39:14.790 line:84%
of the hacienda
or the plantation

00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:21.570 line:84%
have some what we call provision
grounds, some "konoko."

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:25.020 line:84%
Where they won't pay you, they
have you working on the konoko.

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:28.650 line:84%
OK, now you're on the konoko,
on the provision grounds.

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:32.340 line:84%
You're going to organize
the lakou, the yard.

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:35.550 line:84%
You're going to organize
a system of solidarity

00:39:35.550 --> 00:39:38.190 line:84%
and reciprocity where
you protect your people.

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:43.620 line:84%
So you're making use of one
move of the dominant system

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:45.480 line:90%
for your own ends.

00:39:45.480 --> 00:39:50.730 line:84%
We call that you serve, you
utilize the dominant system

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:52.140 line:90%
to serve your own ends.

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:57.120 line:84%
So therefore, the
counter-plantation system

00:39:57.120 --> 00:40:00.570 line:84%
is born within the plantation
system, within the hacienda.

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:05.730 line:84%
And when 1791-- let's
say, 117, [INAUDIBLE]----

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:08.460 line:90%
17-- [INAUDIBLE] '89.

00:40:08.460 --> 00:40:10.410 line:84%
When the French
Revolution started

00:40:10.410 --> 00:40:12.930 line:84%
and the planters,
the "hacenderos,"

00:40:12.930 --> 00:40:18.720 line:84%
start fighting each other, you
start developing your konokos.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:21.060 line:84%
And this is when the
nation is going, really,

00:40:21.060 --> 00:40:25.620 line:84%
to take a structure but
on a national level.

00:40:25.620 --> 00:40:31.020 line:84%
And since I'm there,
what is important is that

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:34.470 line:84%
the counter-plantation
system, the villages--

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:36.960 line:90%
it's a village system.

00:40:36.960 --> 00:40:40.030 line:90%
The villages are isolated.

00:40:40.030 --> 00:40:41.650 line:90%
They are microclimates.

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:42.850 line:90%
They produce certain goods.

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:45.100 line:84%
If you are in the mountain,
you can have spices,

00:40:45.100 --> 00:40:47.040 line:90%
you can have fruits, et cetera.

00:40:47.040 --> 00:40:48.790 line:84%
If you're in the
flatland, you have mango,

00:40:48.790 --> 00:40:49.960 line:90%
you have banana, et cetera.

00:40:49.960 --> 00:40:51.860 line:84%
In the mountain,
you have coffee.

00:40:51.860 --> 00:40:54.640 line:90%
So you have to exchange, no?

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:56.770 line:84%
And you are going
to create, this

00:40:56.770 --> 00:40:58.660 line:84%
is our case, the
internal market,

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:00.890 line:84%
because there was no
internal market in term

00:41:00.890 --> 00:41:02.230 line:90%
of colonial time.

00:41:02.230 --> 00:41:06.190 line:84%
What was important was only
the export of the commodity.

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:08.350 line:84%
So you create the
counter-plantation

00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:10.240 line:90%
you create the internal market.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:13.830 line:90%
And who manage internal market?

00:41:13.830 --> 00:41:15.930 line:90%
The female higglers.

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:19.410 line:84%
The woman was going to tide
of the nation, the women

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:20.910 line:84%
the tide of the
nation, not the men.

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:22.285 line:84%
The men can work,
whatever it is.

00:41:22.285 --> 00:41:26.490 line:84%
But those who are responsible
to moving goods and moving money

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:29.490 line:90%
are female up to now.

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:32.340 line:84%
Your parents when
they send money home,

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:34.080 line:90%
they didn't send it to any man.

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:37.650 line:84%
They send it to a woman,
because she manage money.

00:41:37.650 --> 00:41:39.210 line:90%
She manage resources.

00:41:39.210 --> 00:41:42.810 line:84%
So she will be
the one who will--

00:41:42.810 --> 00:41:44.910 line:84%
tied up-- what I call
the "twa wòch dife"--

00:41:44.910 --> 00:41:47.820 line:84%
the three pillars of
the nation-- community,

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:50.010 line:90%
internal market, and women.

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:51.660 line:90%
But what tied them up?

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:53.160 line:90%
Creole language.

00:41:53.160 --> 00:41:54.060 line:90%
You see what I mean?

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:56.370 line:84%
You don't see a higgler
speaking standard English.

00:41:56.370 --> 00:41:57.040 line:90%
[LAUGHS] No.

00:41:57.040 --> 00:41:57.540 line:90%
No.

00:41:57.540 --> 00:42:00.030 line:84%
Higglers speak rough
Jamaican English.

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:03.000 line:84%
And this is what is
sustaining the nation.

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:05.503 line:84%
On top of that, an
oligarchy is going to--

00:42:05.503 --> 00:42:07.545 line:84%
OK, now give me the second
part of your question.

00:42:07.545 --> 00:42:08.180 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:09.300 line:84%
STUDENT: So my second
question is just like--

00:42:09.300 --> 00:42:10.967 line:84%
I mean, you kind of
answered it already.

00:42:10.967 --> 00:42:13.530 line:84%
But how successful is the
counter-plantation system?

00:42:13.530 --> 00:42:16.500 line:84%
And are we measuring
success on the basis

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:19.352 line:84%
of do our people
remember our history,

00:42:19.352 --> 00:42:20.310 line:90%
are they knowledgeable?

00:42:20.310 --> 00:42:22.260 line:84%
Or are we measuring
on how we manage

00:42:22.260 --> 00:42:24.780 line:84%
to dismantle the
capitalistic society that

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:27.180 line:84%
was established in the Pearl
of the Antilles or Haiti?

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:28.260 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Wonderful.

00:42:28.260 --> 00:42:35.560 line:84%
The counter-plantation system
explains the whole 19th century

00:42:35.560 --> 00:42:37.700 line:90%
Haiti, no?

00:42:37.700 --> 00:42:41.960 line:90%
How we can measure his success?

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:47.310 line:84%
We started independence
with maximum 350,000 people.

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:51.970 line:84%
We ended up the 19th century
with 2 million people.

00:42:51.970 --> 00:42:53.080 line:90%
We multiplied.

00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:55.975 line:84%
But this is not codified
by the government.

00:42:55.975 --> 00:42:58.450 line:84%
Government want to
recreate the plantation,

00:42:58.450 --> 00:43:00.310 line:90%
want to recreate haciendas.

00:43:00.310 --> 00:43:01.730 line:90%
They will not talk about that.

00:43:01.730 --> 00:43:04.000 line:84%
But the fact of the
matter is we did that.

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:08.590 line:84%
Not only did we--
did we do that, we--

00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:11.110 line:90%
I mean, the Haitians, real--

00:43:11.110 --> 00:43:13.660 line:84%
I call them Guinean
Haitians, no.

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:21.600 line:84%
The Haitian, real Haitians
start absorbing the oligarchy,

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:24.150 line:84%
because the oligarchy
has no use for French,

00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:26.460 line:84%
because they had no
relation, nor religion.

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:28.440 line:90%
So they started speaking Kreyòl.

00:43:28.440 --> 00:43:29.490 line:90%
And they had no--

00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:34.680 line:84%
it isn't until 1860
that the Catholic Church

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:36.360 line:84%
acknowledged the
existence of Haiti

00:43:36.360 --> 00:43:39.370 line:84%
and started sending priests
and 12 or 15, whatever it is.

00:43:39.370 --> 00:43:44.490 line:84%
So the people have to use in the
spirituality of Obeah or Vodou.

00:43:44.490 --> 00:43:46.920 line:90%
So they start being absorbed by.

00:43:46.920 --> 00:43:48.930 line:90%
Now what makes that possible?

00:43:48.930 --> 00:43:51.360 line:84%
It's not that we
are so special, no.

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:54.870 line:84%
At that time, the big
empires of the West

00:43:54.870 --> 00:43:57.960 line:84%
were entering in the
imperial colonial time.

00:43:57.960 --> 00:43:59.815 line:84%
They are conquering
Africa, conquering

00:43:59.815 --> 00:44:00.940 line:90%
the [INAUDIBLE], et cetera.

00:44:00.940 --> 00:44:03.600 line:84%
So they could do--
the Caribbean economy

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:05.880 line:90%
becomes totally secondary.

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:08.970 line:84%
And you had beet sugar,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:10.980 line:84%
so they forget about
us, thanks God!

00:44:10.980 --> 00:44:13.500 line:90%
So we could develop.

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:16.860 line:84%
So for instance, if you
take the case of, let's say,

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:19.487 line:84%
to come back to Barbados--
boy, a good thing there is no

00:44:19.487 --> 00:44:20.070 line:90%
[Bajan?] here.

00:44:20.070 --> 00:44:23.340 line:84%
You will challenge me, stop
thinking about Barbados.

00:44:23.340 --> 00:44:27.060 line:84%
But in Barbados for
instance, the planters

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:29.010 line:90%
had a hold on the land.

00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:33.720 line:84%
And the production of
sugarcane after emancipation

00:44:33.720 --> 00:44:36.060 line:90%
was higher than before.

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:39.510 line:84%
To tell you the level of
exploitation of the Bajans,

00:44:39.510 --> 00:44:45.210 line:84%
to such an extent they were so
attacked by hunger that they

00:44:45.210 --> 00:44:50.190 line:84%
start migrate to Guyana and
then to build the Panama Canal,

00:44:50.190 --> 00:44:53.910 line:84%
same thing with Martinique
where the planters could

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:55.440 line:90%
have a hold on the land.

00:44:55.440 --> 00:45:00.300 line:84%
So they had to
migrate outside, no?

00:45:00.300 --> 00:45:03.600 line:84%
Not only that, the migration
to the Panama Canal,

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:07.170 line:84%
to the oil field of
Venezuela, et cetera,

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:11.280 line:84%
was such that some countries in
the Caribbean had no more men.

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:15.600 line:84%
You have only male,
old male, kids.

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:17.290 line:90%
And a set of--

00:45:17.290 --> 00:45:19.830 line:84%
Eric WIlliams even points that
out, homosexuality started

00:45:19.830 --> 00:45:23.310 line:84%
developing to such an
extent for instance,

00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:26.130 line:84%
in the St Lucia,
the word "zanmi,"

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:28.380 line:90%
"friend," means "lesbian."

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:32.550 line:84%
I was making studies in Creole,
no, in Creole Trinidadian.

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:35.820 line:84%
And I said, oh, and so and
so is my zanmi, my friend.

00:45:35.820 --> 00:45:38.220 line:84%
And the St. Lucian said,
you can't say that.

00:45:38.220 --> 00:45:40.230 line:84%
You can't say-- he
can't be your friend.

00:45:40.230 --> 00:45:41.100 line:90%
And I say, why?

00:45:41.100 --> 00:45:44.670 line:84%
Friend means lesbian, because
the word become normal.

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:46.470 line:84%
And it's not that
lesbian [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:51.420 line:90%
you to deface or to diminish.

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:53.040 line:90%
No, no, no, no, that's normal.

00:45:53.040 --> 00:45:54.450 line:90%
The two ladies live together.

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:56.340 line:90%
They are zanmi.

00:45:56.340 --> 00:46:00.450 line:84%
That was normal, because there
was no male figure, adult male.

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.180 line:84%
In several islands-- Eric
Williams even mentioned that

00:46:03.180 --> 00:46:06.270 line:84%
in some Caribbean
islands that regret--

00:46:06.270 --> 00:46:09.800 line:84%
I mean, their populations
started diminishing.

00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:11.640 line:84%
I mean, in the case
of Jamaica, this

00:46:11.640 --> 00:46:14.370 line:84%
is the moment of the
migration toward Costa Rica,

00:46:14.370 --> 00:46:17.010 line:84%
toward the movement
of Marcus Garvey,

00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:21.030 line:84%
because there is no
way you can attack

00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:25.470 line:90%
the land of the British owners.

00:46:25.470 --> 00:46:28.230 line:84%
In the case, of Haiti
we didn't go frontally

00:46:28.230 --> 00:46:32.070 line:90%
toward the land property.

00:46:32.070 --> 00:46:34.500 line:90%
We start occupying it.

00:46:34.500 --> 00:46:37.320 line:84%
And the state didn't
have strength enough

00:46:37.320 --> 00:46:40.020 line:90%
to kick us out of the land.

00:46:40.020 --> 00:46:44.640 line:84%
There are several elements
there I could-- well, in the DR,

00:46:44.640 --> 00:46:46.860 line:84%
I think something
like that happened,

00:46:46.860 --> 00:46:50.920 line:84%
because plantation really
started with Trujillo.

00:46:50.920 --> 00:46:51.720 line:90%
The Americans came.

00:46:51.720 --> 00:46:55.680 line:84%
And they needed to
heal-- to help them.

00:46:55.680 --> 00:46:58.080 line:90%
And you had land.

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:02.550 line:84%
Not only that, the
Dominican Republic I think

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:09.570 line:84%
developed a set of cattle
raising to complete,

00:47:09.570 --> 00:47:15.600 line:84%
to serve the part of colonial
French plantation system.

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:17.970 line:90%
They will sell cattle, no?

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:20.640 line:84%
So there is a whole
tradition of cattle raising.

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:23.790 line:84%
And when Haitians
start their migration,

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:27.660 line:84%
they start since they were
basically agriculturalists,

00:47:27.660 --> 00:47:29.700 line:84%
you see, peasants,
they start planting.

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:33.480 line:84%
And this annoyed Trujillo,
because anything he

00:47:33.480 --> 00:47:35.400 line:90%
made to occupy this land--

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:38.430 line:84%
if he gave land, the Dominicans
go toward cattle raising.

00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:41.820 line:84%
And the one who was taking
profit of the land given

00:47:41.820 --> 00:47:43.980 line:90%
was Haitian origin people.

00:47:43.980 --> 00:47:45.720 line:84%
That was their
tradition, et cetera,

00:47:45.720 --> 00:47:48.900 line:84%
that created certain
conflicts with the state,

00:47:48.900 --> 00:47:52.950 line:84%
because the capital of
the [INAUDIBLE] Eastern

00:47:52.950 --> 00:47:56.430 line:84%
part of the country, not the
central part of the country.

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:58.440 line:84%
And incidentally,
I don't know if you

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:05.540 line:84%
know that most of our heroes
of this period were from--

00:48:05.540 --> 00:48:07.530 line:84%
if not from the Dominican,
from the border--

00:48:07.530 --> 00:48:10.070 line:90%
Hinche, these-- Lascahobas--

00:48:10.070 --> 00:48:12.890 line:84%
This part in-between the
two countries, and this

00:48:12.890 --> 00:48:19.550 line:84%
is where we had most strongest
opponent to US occupation.

00:48:19.550 --> 00:48:22.940 line:84%
Yes, indeed, Charlemagne
Péralte, the name is "Peralta,"

00:48:22.940 --> 00:48:23.940 line:90%
in fact, not "Péralte."

00:48:23.940 --> 00:48:25.190 line:90%
[LAUGHS] You know what I mean?

00:48:25.190 --> 00:48:26.240 line:90%
We say "Péralte."

00:48:26.240 --> 00:48:31.400 line:84%
But he's "Peralta,"
et cetera, et cetera.

00:48:31.400 --> 00:48:33.750 line:84%
Did I answer your
second question?

00:48:33.750 --> 00:48:35.040 line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah, thank you.

00:48:35.040 --> 00:48:36.600 line:90%
STUDENT: I had two questions.

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:39.080 line:84%
One of them is based on the
counter-plantation system.

00:48:39.080 --> 00:48:41.150 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No,
no, remove your mask.

00:48:41.150 --> 00:48:42.660 line:90%
STUDENT: I had two questions.

00:48:42.660 --> 00:48:45.230 line:84%
One of them is based on the
counter-plantation system.

00:48:45.230 --> 00:48:49.460 line:84%
But my question was, would you
say that the counter-plantation

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:56.600 line:84%
system was an idea given to
the enslaved by the colonizers

00:48:56.600 --> 00:48:57.590 line:90%
given that--

00:48:57.590 --> 00:49:00.140 line:84%
because I know in
history books, they

00:49:00.140 --> 00:49:01.850 line:84%
tell you that the
colonizers made

00:49:01.850 --> 00:49:04.950 line:84%
the enslaved find their own
food to sustain themselves.

00:49:04.950 --> 00:49:06.410 line:84%
So I was wondering
is that, do you

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:09.140 line:84%
think that's an idea that
they were given in a sense?

00:49:09.140 --> 00:49:12.788 line:90%
Or was that just a byproduct?

00:49:12.788 --> 00:49:13.830 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: That's one?

00:49:13.830 --> 00:49:15.038 line:90%
STUDENT: That's one question.

00:49:15.038 --> 00:49:16.860 line:84%
And the second
question is, while I

00:49:16.860 --> 00:49:18.780 line:84%
was reading the
chapter I remember

00:49:18.780 --> 00:49:20.520 line:90%
the podcast I was listening to.

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:24.720 line:84%
And one of the guests was
saying that they believe

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:28.080 line:84%
that Haiti should
have waited before,

00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:30.840 line:84%
that Haiti should not have
done the revolution when

00:49:30.840 --> 00:49:31.380 line:90%
they did it.

00:49:31.380 --> 00:49:31.935 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: They
were too early.

00:49:31.935 --> 00:49:32.435 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:32.435 --> 00:49:34.990 line:84%
STUDENT: Right,
so my question is,

00:49:34.990 --> 00:49:36.310 line:90%
what do you think about that?

00:49:36.310 --> 00:49:39.540 line:84%
Because in my opinion, I
could see if they had waited,

00:49:39.540 --> 00:49:44.580 line:84%
it would change the trajectory
of the slave revolutions

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:45.880 line:90%
that came after.

00:49:45.880 --> 00:49:48.408 line:84%
But I just wanted to know
what you thought about that.

00:49:48.408 --> 00:49:49.575 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: OK, too early.

00:49:49.575 --> 00:49:50.075 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:50.075 --> 00:49:55.230 line:84%
Listen, first of all, the
counter-plantation system,

00:49:55.230 --> 00:49:58.140 line:84%
I call it
counter-plantation system

00:49:58.140 --> 00:50:03.360 line:84%
because it's not the classical
peasantry, simply for that,

00:50:03.360 --> 00:50:07.260 line:84%
because the classical
peasantry is self-sufficient.

00:50:07.260 --> 00:50:09.810 line:84%
If you take, for instance,
Mesoamerica or even

00:50:09.810 --> 00:50:13.740 line:84%
Central Europe, the peasants
produce even their instrument

00:50:13.740 --> 00:50:16.200 line:84%
of production, of the-- yes,
instrument of production,

00:50:16.200 --> 00:50:18.480 line:90%
like Cuba, no?

00:50:18.480 --> 00:50:20.700 line:84%
Not only that, they
have even the textile.

00:50:20.700 --> 00:50:23.400 line:84%
There is no more
sophisticated textile

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:25.470 line:90%
than the Guatemalan textile.

00:50:25.470 --> 00:50:28.410 line:84%
They weave their thing
into something great.

00:50:28.410 --> 00:50:31.770 line:84%
But if you are an enslaved,
there is a certain number

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:35.940 line:84%
of things you cannot produce
yourself, like button,

00:50:35.940 --> 00:50:42.070 line:84%
an instrument, machete,
because you don't have any--

00:50:42.070 --> 00:50:46.300 line:84%
while the Africans were
very skillful in dealing

00:50:46.300 --> 00:50:49.660 line:84%
with iron, but in
the Caribbean, where

00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:50.860 line:90%
are you going to find iron?

00:50:50.860 --> 00:50:53.740 line:84%
So you have a
"peasantry," in quote,

00:50:53.740 --> 00:50:58.450 line:84%
that must produce an excess to
exchange for the other good.

00:50:58.450 --> 00:51:00.100 line:90%
So it's not exactly a peasant.

00:51:00.100 --> 00:51:02.540 line:84%
It's why I call it the
counter-plantation system.

00:51:02.540 --> 00:51:05.860 line:84%
Now, I don't think the system
comes from the-- that has

00:51:05.860 --> 00:51:08.770 line:84%
nothing to do with the planters,
because the planter cannot

00:51:08.770 --> 00:51:15.880 line:84%
imagine a type of relation,
family relation like we have.

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:18.470 line:84%
First of all, to understand
really our family,

00:51:18.470 --> 00:51:20.440 line:84%
we must take into
account the fact

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:23.710 line:90%
that the slave has no gender.

00:51:23.710 --> 00:51:28.390 line:84%
They have sex but no gender,
like cattle have no gender.

00:51:28.390 --> 00:51:32.050 line:84%
So the first creation of the
counter-plantation system

00:51:32.050 --> 00:51:34.030 line:90%
will be the invention of women.

00:51:34.030 --> 00:51:35.410 line:90%
We create our women.

00:51:35.410 --> 00:51:36.640 line:90%
We create our agenda.

00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:37.810 line:90%
We create our men.

00:51:37.810 --> 00:51:41.590 line:84%
We have to imagine
a form of structure

00:51:41.590 --> 00:51:44.590 line:84%
where that female and
that male can function.

00:51:44.590 --> 00:51:47.020 line:84%
It did not function
under slavery.

00:51:47.020 --> 00:51:49.700 line:84%
It function in the
counter-plantation system.

00:51:49.700 --> 00:51:55.480 line:84%
Now, we create also the form
of a pairing--"mariage," no?--

00:51:55.480 --> 00:52:02.020 line:84%
the formal-- because
do we have the mono--

00:52:02.020 --> 00:52:02.800 line:90%
bi-- I don't know.

00:52:02.800 --> 00:52:03.342 line:90%
Mono parent--

00:52:03.342 --> 00:52:04.175 line:90%
STUDENT: Monogamous?

00:52:04.175 --> 00:52:05.260 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Monogamy?

00:52:05.260 --> 00:52:06.730 line:90%
Do we have polygamy?

00:52:06.730 --> 00:52:08.140 line:90%
Do we have--

00:52:08.140 --> 00:52:12.130 line:84%
I mean, that's-- up to now,
we cannot solve that question.

00:52:12.130 --> 00:52:14.110 line:90%
Let me tell you an anecdote.

00:52:14.110 --> 00:52:15.730 line:90%
STUDENT: Nobody can.

00:52:15.730 --> 00:52:17.563 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Particularly
in our countries.

00:52:17.563 --> 00:52:22.600 line:84%
For instance, I was telling
my wife, my companion

00:52:22.600 --> 00:52:25.780 line:84%
that the young
lady walking by me

00:52:25.780 --> 00:52:28.990 line:84%
is telling that
so-and-so of his friend

00:52:28.990 --> 00:52:32.950 line:84%
is not there, because
the husband went

00:52:32.950 --> 00:52:37.550 line:90%
to visit his married woman.

00:52:37.550 --> 00:52:41.990 line:84%
And also, her
friend is available.

00:52:41.990 --> 00:52:44.480 line:84%
She can visit her friend,
because the husband

00:52:44.480 --> 00:52:46.850 line:84%
of her friend go to
see her married woman.

00:52:46.850 --> 00:52:49.257 line:84%
So I tell my wife, "If I
say you my married woman,

00:52:49.257 --> 00:52:50.090 line:90%
what would you say?"

00:52:50.090 --> 00:52:50.965 line:90%
She say, [INAUDIBLE].

00:52:50.965 --> 00:52:55.550 line:84%
[LAUGHS] because
the fact of assuming

00:52:55.550 --> 00:52:58.250 line:84%
that the companion
has a married woman

00:52:58.250 --> 00:53:00.530 line:90%
means that they are polygamous.

00:53:00.530 --> 00:53:03.770 line:84%
And she is not-- she doesn't
feel any bad about that.

00:53:03.770 --> 00:53:05.270 line:90%
She's living in polygamy.

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:09.110 line:84%
And the first lady who married
the man is a married woman.

00:53:09.110 --> 00:53:10.850 line:90%
And she is also the woman.

00:53:10.850 --> 00:53:15.500 line:84%
And for the married woman, don't
you believe that this means--

00:53:15.500 --> 00:53:17.750 line:84%
for the ladies, that
means-- no, no, that

00:53:17.750 --> 00:53:19.610 line:84%
means the fellow is
not there all the time

00:53:19.610 --> 00:53:21.350 line:90%
to bother them, period.

00:53:21.350 --> 00:53:22.640 line:90%
They can do their thing.

00:53:22.640 --> 00:53:24.860 line:90%
They organize their life.

00:53:24.860 --> 00:53:29.570 line:84%
Simply in the house, some
sexual partner when they need

00:53:29.570 --> 00:53:31.400 line:84%
or where they
arrange themselves.

00:53:31.400 --> 00:53:36.720 line:84%
It's a different set,
conception of family.

00:53:36.720 --> 00:53:39.410 line:84%
Not only that, when
you have a quarrel,

00:53:39.410 --> 00:53:41.900 line:84%
you have conflicts,
the lady, who

00:53:41.900 --> 00:53:45.020 line:84%
is the weakest
person in the couple,

00:53:45.020 --> 00:53:47.810 line:84%
she is not going toward
the husband to help her.

00:53:47.810 --> 00:53:51.660 line:84%
She going to the
eldest brother, she

00:53:51.660 --> 00:53:53.970 line:84%
look in her family who
will protect her, not

00:53:53.970 --> 00:53:57.330 line:90%
in the family of the husband.

00:53:57.330 --> 00:54:01.170 line:84%
She may, but that's
not necessary.

00:54:01.170 --> 00:54:03.470 line:84%
What is it say is
that the husband--

00:54:03.470 --> 00:54:05.040 line:84%
I mean, sorry, the
husband-- the head

00:54:05.040 --> 00:54:06.930 line:90%
of the fam-- of her family.

00:54:06.930 --> 00:54:11.130 line:84%
The eldest brother or whoever
is the head must come out.

00:54:11.130 --> 00:54:13.170 line:84%
If the husband come,
that will help.

00:54:13.170 --> 00:54:14.340 line:90%
But that's not the point.

00:54:14.340 --> 00:54:15.240 line:90%
She goes to--

00:54:15.240 --> 00:54:18.840 line:84%
So we have another
concept, organization,

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:21.150 line:84%
which is not
codified, because we

00:54:21.150 --> 00:54:24.600 line:84%
are so brainwashed by
the Catholic Church.

00:54:24.600 --> 00:54:27.180 line:90%
We're looking for a--

00:54:27.180 --> 00:54:28.890 line:90%
couples all over.

00:54:28.890 --> 00:54:31.182 line:84%
And we're saying, the
men are so promiscuous.

00:54:31.182 --> 00:54:32.640 line:84%
They don't take
care of their kids.

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:34.980 line:90%
It's not the father.

00:54:34.980 --> 00:54:37.780 line:84%
Well, I won't give
more anecdote.

00:54:37.780 --> 00:54:40.070 line:84%
I was going to give
you another one, but--

00:54:40.070 --> 00:54:41.820 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:43.320 line:90%
The other one is the following.

00:54:43.320 --> 00:54:47.400 line:84%
I was teaching in
Congo with my wife.

00:54:47.400 --> 00:54:49.470 line:90%
We were both teachers.

00:54:49.470 --> 00:54:53.760 line:84%
And one of the essays
that my wife received

00:54:53.760 --> 00:54:58.300 line:84%
said, my eldest father was
sleeping under the tree.

00:54:58.300 --> 00:54:59.500 line:90%
My wife say, what's that?

00:54:59.500 --> 00:55:01.460 line:84%
So we had the Congolese
living with us.

00:55:01.460 --> 00:55:04.060 line:84%
He said, listen, what
is an eldest father?

00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:06.040 line:90%
He has a younger one?

00:55:06.040 --> 00:55:08.590 line:84%
And the Congolese start
laughing, like hell,

00:55:08.590 --> 00:55:10.720 line:84%
he takes five minutes
before he answered.

00:55:10.720 --> 00:55:12.855 line:84%
He says, yes, because
the word for "father"

00:55:12.855 --> 00:55:13.855 line:90%
is the same for "uncle."

00:55:13.855 --> 00:55:22.990 line:84%
[LAUGHS] That means the
eldest brother of his mother

00:55:22.990 --> 00:55:24.070 line:90%
as a "father."

00:55:24.070 --> 00:55:27.610 line:84%
And in Haiti, everybody
calls me "father."

00:55:27.610 --> 00:55:32.110 line:84%
When I go Southern Mexico, they
call me "abuelo," grandfather.

00:55:32.110 --> 00:55:33.520 line:90%
Everybody calls me "abuelo"--

00:55:33.520 --> 00:55:37.150 line:84%
and "mi abuelo" let me do
that, in Haiti, father--

00:55:37.150 --> 00:55:39.070 line:84%
to such an extent
the police stop me

00:55:39.070 --> 00:55:42.730 line:84%
and say, "Father,
give me your license."

00:55:42.730 --> 00:55:45.640 line:90%
I say, "Just a minute, my son."

00:55:45.640 --> 00:55:46.805 line:90%
"How come you call me son?"

00:55:46.805 --> 00:55:48.430 line:84%
I say, "But you just
called me father."

00:55:48.430 --> 00:55:50.020 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:56.620 line:84%
You see, I mean,
these are definitions

00:55:56.620 --> 00:55:58.510 line:90%
that are contextual.

00:55:58.510 --> 00:56:00.130 line:90%
And we use it.

00:56:00.130 --> 00:56:01.635 line:90%
But this is not codified.

00:56:01.635 --> 00:56:03.010 line:84%
And the state do
not want to hear

00:56:03.010 --> 00:56:04.385 line:84%
from that, because
the state have

00:56:04.385 --> 00:56:08.390 line:84%
to be at par with the
international community.

00:56:08.390 --> 00:56:10.720 line:84%
And there is no way you
make them respect that.

00:56:10.720 --> 00:56:13.660 line:84%
And I give this story
to Professor DeGraff

00:56:13.660 --> 00:56:18.100 line:84%
of that friend of mine who is a
member of the great commission

00:56:18.100 --> 00:56:19.450 line:90%
of human rights.

00:56:19.450 --> 00:56:23.200 line:84%
Now, when we say they should
make laws for the people who

00:56:23.200 --> 00:56:28.000 line:84%
shack up, he told me shacking
up cannot be object of law

00:56:28.000 --> 00:56:30.544 line:90%
because it is a crime.

00:56:30.544 --> 00:56:31.460 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

00:56:31.460 --> 00:56:35.537 line:84%
I mean, you can imagine
a lawyer saying that.

00:56:35.537 --> 00:56:37.120 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: A
human-rights lawyer?

00:56:37.120 --> 00:56:38.245 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Human rights.

00:56:38.245 --> 00:56:40.780 line:84%
Somebody of my age, a
little bit younger than me--

00:56:40.780 --> 00:56:44.010 line:84%
I mean, but this is what
he received at school.

00:56:44.010 --> 00:56:46.810 line:84%
STUDENT: But are we also
talking about the difference

00:56:46.810 --> 00:56:49.810 line:84%
between citizenship,
being a citizen, which

00:56:49.810 --> 00:56:51.610 line:84%
is what the state
wants us to be,

00:56:51.610 --> 00:56:55.645 line:84%
"citizens," and then being
a people of the lakou

00:56:55.645 --> 00:56:56.350 line:90%
or community--

00:56:56.350 --> 00:56:57.670 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yes, exactly.

00:56:57.670 --> 00:57:00.910 line:84%
STUDENT: Of like calling you
"father," not calling you

00:57:00.910 --> 00:57:01.880 line:90%
"citizen."

00:57:01.880 --> 00:57:05.230 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: "Citizen," in
our case, in our tradition,

00:57:05.230 --> 00:57:10.450 line:84%
is the land owner who has a
certain number of enslaved.

00:57:10.450 --> 00:57:12.640 line:90%
Those are "active citizens."

00:57:12.640 --> 00:57:14.856 line:84%
And the others are
"passive citizens."

00:57:14.856 --> 00:57:15.690 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:15.690 --> 00:57:17.230 line:84%
STUDENT: That's
what we're all being

00:57:17.230 --> 00:57:20.590 line:84%
pushed to become, less
of community people

00:57:20.590 --> 00:57:21.715 line:90%
and more of citizens than--

00:57:21.715 --> 00:57:23.340 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No,
but the people do not

00:57:23.340 --> 00:57:24.600 line:90%
want to be "citizens" either.

00:57:24.600 --> 00:57:25.870 line:90%
They do not want the state.

00:57:25.870 --> 00:57:27.850 line:84%
The state never did
anything for them

00:57:27.850 --> 00:57:30.520 line:84%
since they exist,
they start existing.

00:57:30.520 --> 00:57:32.600 line:84%
I mean, they don't
want to participate.

00:57:32.600 --> 00:57:34.420 line:84%
STUDENT: I hope so,
but I mean, more people

00:57:34.420 --> 00:57:37.825 line:84%
want to be citizens is
a problem in the world.

00:57:37.825 --> 00:57:38.700 line:90%
I think the concept--

00:57:38.700 --> 00:57:41.710 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No, I'm
talking about local history.

00:57:41.710 --> 00:57:43.200 line:90%
For in the world, I don't know.

00:57:43.200 --> 00:57:45.492 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: It is a
question that Iana asked about.

00:57:45.492 --> 00:57:46.860 line:90%
Was it too early?

00:57:46.860 --> 00:57:49.330 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Oh yeah, that's
a really important question.

00:57:49.330 --> 00:57:52.830 line:84%
OK, first of all, at
least in sociology,

00:57:52.830 --> 00:57:54.780 line:84%
you don't decide
what's going to happen.

00:57:54.780 --> 00:57:56.790 line:90%
Society moves.

00:57:56.790 --> 00:57:59.380 line:84%
And we had an opportunity
we went through.

00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:01.500 line:90%
We were not asked to do.

00:58:01.500 --> 00:58:06.150 line:84%
For instance, De Vastey speak
of the "Haitians in spite of"--

00:58:06.150 --> 00:58:08.100 line:90%
the "forced Haitian."

00:58:08.100 --> 00:58:12.330 line:84%
Not talking about the
set of the freed men,

00:58:12.330 --> 00:58:16.290 line:84%
emancipated persons who wanted
to move and to be French

00:58:16.290 --> 00:58:19.860 line:84%
and who actually maintained
France or French up to now

00:58:19.860 --> 00:58:21.870 line:90%
as the key of Haiti.

00:58:21.870 --> 00:58:26.370 line:84%
But Napoleon had decided to
kill them and to deport them.

00:58:26.370 --> 00:58:30.630 line:84%
So they had to fight with
the Maroons for independence.

00:58:30.630 --> 00:58:35.182 line:84%
So he called them
"Les Haïtiens"--

00:58:35.182 --> 00:58:38.250 line:84%
Haitians in spite of
themselves, forced Haitians.

00:58:38.250 --> 00:58:42.330 line:84%
And those Haitians were
very important in the fight

00:58:42.330 --> 00:58:44.730 line:84%
for independence together
with the Maroons.

00:58:44.730 --> 00:58:47.070 line:84%
If not, we will have a modern
society and Bush Negroes

00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:48.000 line:90%
like in Suriname.

00:58:48.000 --> 00:58:51.000 line:84%
And then they would
conquer us again.

00:58:51.000 --> 00:58:52.170 line:90%
So they were important.

00:58:52.170 --> 00:58:56.670 line:84%
Nonetheless, they didn't
choose to fight France.

00:58:56.670 --> 00:58:58.290 line:84%
France forced them
to fight them.

00:58:58.290 --> 00:58:59.310 line:90%
That's one aspect.

00:58:59.310 --> 00:59:03.090 line:84%
Second aspect, which is very,
very, very important, when

00:59:03.090 --> 00:59:05.790 line:84%
most people are saying
Haiti is independent,

00:59:05.790 --> 00:59:09.210 line:84%
was independent too soon--
and not anybody said that.

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:13.080 line:84%
I'm talking about Aimé Césaire
I'm talking about Derek

00:59:13.080 --> 00:59:14.400 line:90%
Walcott.

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:17.740 line:84%
I mean, people who say, how
could Haiti so rich is so poor

00:59:17.740 --> 00:59:18.240 line:90%
now?

00:59:18.240 --> 00:59:19.290 line:90%
That's not possible.

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:21.090 line:84%
They have been
independent too much.

00:59:21.090 --> 00:59:23.730 line:84%
No, well, OK, I'm
not saying what

00:59:23.730 --> 00:59:26.487 line:84%
is the level of living
in the other countries

00:59:26.487 --> 00:59:29.070 line:84%
of the Caribbean, because I can
say the same thing for the US.

00:59:29.070 --> 00:59:31.590 line:84%
I don't think the US
is that good either.

00:59:31.590 --> 00:59:33.480 line:90%
But OK, I grant--

00:59:33.480 --> 00:59:35.190 line:84%
whatever they want,
I grant them that.

00:59:35.190 --> 00:59:38.520 line:84%
But what they do not
take into account

00:59:38.520 --> 00:59:43.560 line:84%
is the fact that the Haitians,
by being independent in 1804,

00:59:43.560 --> 00:59:46.410 line:84%
protected the
Haitian from death.

00:59:46.410 --> 00:59:49.100 line:84%
Let me tell you
the circumstances.

00:59:49.100 --> 00:59:54.380 line:84%
By 1803 or 1802 or
1801-- but anyway,

00:59:54.380 --> 00:59:57.200 line:84%
before Haiti become
independent, Napoleon

00:59:57.200 --> 01:00:00.110 line:84%
gave the order to kill all
the Blacks who were rebel

01:00:00.110 --> 01:00:01.910 line:90%
and to re-people the islands.

01:00:01.910 --> 01:00:03.530 line:90%
But he didn't give the order.

01:00:03.530 --> 01:00:08.480 line:84%
He started implementing
this order in Guadeloupe

01:00:08.480 --> 01:00:10.370 line:90%
by General Richepanse.

01:00:10.370 --> 01:00:13.400 line:84%
He started killing
Blacks, killing them.

01:00:13.400 --> 01:00:17.270 line:84%
And the Guadeloupeans who had
started the revolution, when

01:00:17.270 --> 01:00:20.670 line:84%
they realized they are
dying, they decided,

01:00:20.670 --> 01:00:24.470 line:84%
OK, let's try to live another
day, so we will fight tomorrow.

01:00:24.470 --> 01:00:26.018 line:90%
And they stop fighting.

01:00:26.018 --> 01:00:26.810 line:90%
And they surrender.

01:00:26.810 --> 01:00:29.690 line:84%
And they were re-enslaved
because they were fighting

01:00:29.690 --> 01:00:31.880 line:90%
against re-enslavement.

01:00:31.880 --> 01:00:35.330 line:84%
When they stopped fighting,
they found that 10% of them

01:00:35.330 --> 01:00:37.340 line:90%
were already killed.

01:00:37.340 --> 01:00:39.020 line:90%
So they were about to kill them.

01:00:39.020 --> 01:00:42.320 line:84%
In the case of Haiti,
they start the same thing

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:48.920 line:84%
at such a point that the Poles
who he had sent to fight us--

01:00:48.920 --> 01:00:51.230 line:84%
they were Pol--
they were peasants--

01:00:51.230 --> 01:00:54.050 line:84%
realizing that they say,
no, we are not participating

01:00:54.050 --> 01:00:55.760 line:90%
in killing civilians and kids.

01:00:55.760 --> 01:00:57.320 line:90%
We didn't come here for that.

01:00:57.320 --> 01:00:59.960 line:84%
And they move and fight
with the Haitians.

01:00:59.960 --> 01:01:02.810 line:84%
And this is also another
reason why Dessalines declared

01:01:02.810 --> 01:01:05.013 line:84%
that the Poles were
Black, because this

01:01:05.013 --> 01:01:05.930 line:90%
is what we call Black.

01:01:05.930 --> 01:01:08.810 line:90%
It's not being Black tinted.

01:01:08.810 --> 01:01:12.000 line:90%
OK, so Haiti became independent.

01:01:12.000 --> 01:01:16.655 line:84%
And Napoleon could not kill
those Haitians to have--

01:01:16.655 --> 01:01:18.260 line:90%
too many people, they say.

01:01:18.260 --> 01:01:23.630 line:84%
In the countries that remain
colonies that were not

01:01:23.630 --> 01:01:26.315 line:84%
independent so soon,
as I just told you,

01:01:26.315 --> 01:01:28.670 line:84%
you had to let a set
of out migration,

01:01:28.670 --> 01:01:30.800 line:84%
because you had
hungers in Martinique,

01:01:30.800 --> 01:01:33.650 line:84%
hungers in Barbados,
hungers in-- great hungers,

01:01:33.650 --> 01:01:36.050 line:90%
because the white people--

01:01:36.050 --> 01:01:42.050 line:84%
Barbados is a typical case--
intensified the exploitation

01:01:42.050 --> 01:01:45.050 line:84%
of their former
enslaved and produce

01:01:45.050 --> 01:01:49.280 line:84%
much more without
introducing new technology.

01:01:49.280 --> 01:01:52.850 line:84%
You see, Barbados was
producing more than Cuba

01:01:52.850 --> 01:01:56.490 line:84%
when Cuba had production
with new technology.

01:01:56.490 --> 01:02:00.980 line:84%
So they increased the
exploitation of the Bajans.

01:02:00.980 --> 01:02:03.140 line:90%
And more Bajans die in Barbados.

01:02:03.140 --> 01:02:04.940 line:84%
And more Bajans had
to leave the country

01:02:04.940 --> 01:02:09.240 line:84%
in conditions that were very
similar to the slave trade.

01:02:09.240 --> 01:02:12.320 line:84%
So it is-- you didn't
choose to be independent.

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:13.370 line:90%
The opportunity comes.

01:02:13.370 --> 01:02:14.730 line:90%
There is a crack in the system.

01:02:14.730 --> 01:02:16.130 line:90%
And you move.

01:02:16.130 --> 01:02:18.620 line:90%
And you establish yourself.

01:02:18.620 --> 01:02:20.480 line:90%
But how did you done that?

01:02:20.480 --> 01:02:22.430 line:90%
What would happen?

01:02:22.430 --> 01:02:28.310 line:84%
Because now that
gives us one way out.

01:02:28.310 --> 01:02:30.560 line:90%
Haitians become independent.

01:02:30.560 --> 01:02:33.620 line:84%
We multiply the population, et
cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

01:02:33.620 --> 01:02:38.510 line:84%
But our development is
based on inner management

01:02:38.510 --> 01:02:42.140 line:90%
of our inner system.

01:02:42.140 --> 01:02:45.530 line:84%
While we do not have one
thing the Bajans have

01:02:45.530 --> 01:02:47.570 line:84%
or the Jamaicans
also, they know how

01:02:47.570 --> 01:02:49.370 line:84%
to negotiate with
the other person.

01:02:49.370 --> 01:02:52.160 line:90%
We don't know that up to now.

01:02:52.160 --> 01:02:57.740 line:84%
It is now that we are learning
what happened with the West.

01:02:57.740 --> 01:03:00.170 line:84%
When the Americans
occupied Haiti,

01:03:00.170 --> 01:03:02.960 line:84%
the president of
Haiti, Dartiguenave,

01:03:02.960 --> 01:03:05.900 line:84%
had no idea of what the
Americans were up to.

01:03:05.900 --> 01:03:07.430 line:90%
He thought they came to help us.

01:03:07.430 --> 01:03:09.860 line:84%
He could not imagine
they came to exploit us.

01:03:09.860 --> 01:03:12.950 line:84%
You can imagine the fellow
president of the Republic

01:03:12.950 --> 01:03:15.050 line:90%
has no idea of the occupant.

01:03:15.050 --> 01:03:19.670 line:84%
Not only that, the other
day when the US, no,

01:03:19.670 --> 01:03:28.160 line:84%
the UN head of mission visited
the general headquarters

01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:31.250 line:84%
of the army, in the
general headquarters

01:03:31.250 --> 01:03:34.580 line:84%
of the army, all the big
chiefs of the US occupation

01:03:34.580 --> 01:03:37.520 line:84%
were there as founder
of the Haitian army.

01:03:37.520 --> 01:03:41.090 line:84%
He said, where you have found
an independent country where

01:03:41.090 --> 01:03:45.020 line:84%
the occupant is put as
the creator of the army,

01:03:45.020 --> 01:03:46.640 line:90%
of the national army?

01:03:46.640 --> 01:03:52.130 line:84%
You can imagine
Jamaica's headquarters

01:03:52.130 --> 01:03:55.220 line:84%
of the army with all the British
there who created the army.

01:03:55.220 --> 01:03:58.610 line:84%
For God's sake, at
least take them off.

01:03:58.610 --> 01:04:01.100 line:84%
But in Haiti up to now,
and they had that-- no, not

01:04:01.100 --> 01:04:04.070 line:84%
up to now, because
Aristide ended this army,

01:04:04.070 --> 01:04:07.130 line:90%
but to tell you the situation.

01:04:07.130 --> 01:04:09.950 line:84%
So is Haiti independent
too early or not?

01:04:09.950 --> 01:04:11.990 line:90%
OK, I don't know.

01:04:11.990 --> 01:04:13.970 line:84%
Ask-- as Vastey
would say, as those

01:04:13.970 --> 01:04:19.580 line:84%
who died on the boat
going-- and the life

01:04:19.580 --> 01:04:22.190 line:84%
of the Caribbean
people in Panama

01:04:22.190 --> 01:04:24.230 line:84%
during the construction
of the, like-- there's

01:04:24.230 --> 01:04:29.030 line:84%
a wonderful documentary
made here in the US

01:04:29.030 --> 01:04:34.070 line:84%
on the Caribbean people
building the Panama Canal.

01:04:34.070 --> 01:04:36.835 line:90%
Oh boy, you are to cry.

01:04:36.835 --> 01:04:37.460 line:90%
You are to cry.

01:04:37.460 --> 01:04:40.350 line:84%
And up to now they
are protesting

01:04:40.350 --> 01:04:42.350 line:84%
the way they beat them,
the way they treat them,

01:04:42.350 --> 01:04:47.240 line:84%
how they died, I mean, in
Culebra, building the Panama

01:04:47.240 --> 01:04:52.070 line:84%
Canal, because you should not
forget that the 19th century is

01:04:52.070 --> 01:04:54.530 line:90%
the time of Jim Crow laws here.

01:04:54.530 --> 01:04:57.330 line:84%
And those who are
building the canal,

01:04:57.330 --> 01:05:00.200 line:90%
they are the Jim Crow fellows.

01:05:00.200 --> 01:05:01.490 line:90%
I mean, they are--

01:05:01.490 --> 01:05:04.310 line:84%
just have when the
Black North American,

01:05:04.310 --> 01:05:06.350 line:84%
when African-Americans
start voting,

01:05:06.350 --> 01:05:09.140 line:84%
to give you an idea of what
happened 50 years earlier

01:05:09.140 --> 01:05:12.290 line:84%
in countries that were
not even America--

01:05:12.290 --> 01:05:14.210 line:84%
this is how-- you
know, you can imagine

01:05:14.210 --> 01:05:15.240 line:90%
what was going on there.

01:05:15.240 --> 01:05:19.340 line:84%
Now, so was Haiti
independent too early?

01:05:19.340 --> 01:05:23.660 line:84%
I mean, are we comparing
what is left of Barbados,

01:05:23.660 --> 01:05:25.550 line:84%
because we let a
set of Barbadian

01:05:25.550 --> 01:05:27.740 line:84%
die, because this
is what happened.

01:05:27.740 --> 01:05:31.460 line:84%
Barbados is the country,
at least compared to Haiti,

01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:32.780 line:90%
where even--

01:05:32.780 --> 01:05:38.510 line:84%
everybody has secondary school
diploma, everybody, everybody.

01:05:38.510 --> 01:05:42.170 line:84%
In Haiti, oh, I think we can
count them with your hand.

01:05:42.170 --> 01:05:43.850 line:84%
And those who have
it, we don't know

01:05:43.850 --> 01:05:49.670 line:84%
how bad is that instruction--
in education-- schooling is.

01:05:49.670 --> 01:05:54.950 line:84%
So obviously, this allows
certain regions to go forward.

01:05:54.950 --> 01:05:57.200 line:90%
But how many of them died?

01:05:57.200 --> 01:06:00.560 line:90%
How many of them are not there?

01:06:00.560 --> 01:06:03.530 line:84%
Obviously, I'm sure that
the state will not speak

01:06:03.530 --> 01:06:06.290 line:90%
too much about the suffering.

01:06:06.290 --> 01:06:12.950 line:84%
Martinique knew hunger, while
Haiti was at the time in what

01:06:12.950 --> 01:06:16.350 line:84%
Manigat called-- one of our
historians called ""le bonheur

01:06:16.350 --> 01:06:18.860 line:90%
vivrier," the village happiness.

01:06:18.860 --> 01:06:22.820 line:84%
I mean, we had full-fledged
village happiness.

01:06:22.820 --> 01:06:25.520 line:84%
And the knowledge--
we allowed the state

01:06:25.520 --> 01:06:27.530 line:90%
building one hospital.

01:06:27.530 --> 01:06:30.470 line:84%
Just the knowledge of the
people, which was not too much,

01:06:30.470 --> 01:06:31.380 line:90%
you could go--

01:06:31.380 --> 01:06:34.730 line:84%
But anyway, that was enough
to multiply the population

01:06:34.730 --> 01:06:35.570 line:90%
by five.

01:06:35.570 --> 01:06:40.550 line:84%
And not only that, there is no
country in the whole America

01:06:40.550 --> 01:06:43.220 line:84%
where you have a normal
increase of the population

01:06:43.220 --> 01:06:44.910 line:84%
naturally because
of the population.

01:06:44.910 --> 01:06:46.490 line:84%
All of them increased
the population

01:06:46.490 --> 01:06:48.860 line:84%
through migration
coming from Europe.

01:06:48.860 --> 01:06:52.520 line:84%
And at this 19th century
when they were migrating

01:06:52.520 --> 01:06:56.810 line:84%
toward the Latin
America or South Africa,

01:06:56.810 --> 01:06:58.502 line:84%
et cetera, et cetera,
I want to know

01:06:58.502 --> 01:07:00.710 line:84%
why they were leaving their
country, not because they

01:07:00.710 --> 01:07:02.210 line:90%
have too much.

01:07:02.210 --> 01:07:04.160 line:90%
Why the Italians left Italy?

01:07:04.160 --> 01:07:08.210 line:84%
Why the Spaniard left
Spain, the Vatican City?

01:07:08.210 --> 01:07:13.160 line:84%
Why the Dutch and the British
left in the 19th century

01:07:13.160 --> 01:07:16.040 line:84%
when the Industrial
Revolution is

01:07:16.040 --> 01:07:19.640 line:84%
taking place, when you have
capitalism developing itself

01:07:19.640 --> 01:07:21.500 line:90%
and conquering the whole world?

01:07:21.500 --> 01:07:25.100 line:84%
What was the level of living,
the number of Swedish people

01:07:25.100 --> 01:07:28.390 line:90%
who went from Sweden to the US?

01:07:28.390 --> 01:07:30.100 line:84%
And you can see their
photo and they're

01:07:30.100 --> 01:07:31.900 line:90%
showing they are migrants.

01:07:31.900 --> 01:07:36.290 line:84%
Killing Indians, OK, but
listen, if you are so well-fed

01:07:36.290 --> 01:07:42.910 line:84%
in Swede, you are not coming
here to kill-- in Sweden--

01:07:42.910 --> 01:07:43.780 line:90%
to kill Indians.

01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:45.800 line:90%
Stay home.

01:07:45.800 --> 01:07:48.910 line:84%
So all the things, they are
not spoken, this is not spoken.

01:07:48.910 --> 01:07:52.600 line:84%
And in that time, the
Haitian will multiply.

01:07:52.600 --> 01:07:56.180 line:84%
This is another idea
of wealth, to answer.

01:07:56.180 --> 01:07:57.590 line:90%
You see the wealth?

01:07:57.590 --> 01:07:59.840 line:90%
What is wealth?

01:07:59.840 --> 01:08:00.990 line:90%
STUDENT: My question?

01:08:00.990 --> 01:08:04.910 line:84%
So I'll say my general
question and then

01:08:04.910 --> 01:08:07.260 line:84%
give a little bit of
background for how I got there.

01:08:07.260 --> 01:08:09.650 line:90%
But it seems very general.

01:08:09.650 --> 01:08:12.260 line:84%
But my question is
like, what is your why.

01:08:12.260 --> 01:08:14.548 line:84%
And the way I got to
that was I think--

01:08:14.548 --> 01:08:16.340 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Do you
say, what is your why?

01:08:16.340 --> 01:08:18.649 line:84%
STUDENT: Your why,
like why you're

01:08:18.649 --> 01:08:22.640 line:84%
doing this, why you have this
power, and things like this.

01:08:22.640 --> 01:08:24.109 line:84%
The way I got to
this question is

01:08:24.109 --> 01:08:26.240 line:84%
I think every week
we do readings.

01:08:26.240 --> 01:08:27.500 line:90%
We come, someone presents.

01:08:27.500 --> 01:08:29.300 line:84%
And while it is based
on the readings,

01:08:29.300 --> 01:08:31.080 line:84%
it's so based on
themselves as well.

01:08:31.080 --> 01:08:32.810 line:84%
And like I said,
I've learned so much

01:08:32.810 --> 01:08:35.660 line:84%
about all the people in this
class and what is their why,

01:08:35.660 --> 01:08:37.910 line:84%
and like why they're
here, what they

01:08:37.910 --> 01:08:39.960 line:84%
take from like every
reading and stuff like that.

01:08:39.960 --> 01:08:43.880 line:84%
So I wanted to know like
specifically and personally

01:08:43.880 --> 01:08:44.835 line:90%
what is your why.

01:08:44.835 --> 01:08:45.710 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: My why?

01:08:45.710 --> 01:08:46.293 line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

01:08:46.293 --> 01:08:48.622 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:08:48.622 --> 01:08:49.580 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: You mean.

01:08:49.580 --> 01:08:50.450 line:90%
STUDENT: I love your question.

01:08:50.450 --> 01:08:52.160 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: You
mean, you are mean.

01:08:52.160 --> 01:08:53.060 line:90%
Let me tell you.

01:08:53.060 --> 01:08:53.720 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: That's
a good question.

01:08:53.720 --> 01:08:54.350 line:90%
I love it.

01:08:54.350 --> 01:08:57.229 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yeah, because
the colonial study implies

01:08:57.229 --> 01:08:59.300 line:90%
that you position yourself.

01:08:59.300 --> 01:09:01.508 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: That's right,
absolutely, absolutely.

01:09:01.508 --> 01:09:02.842 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: It's a long story.

01:09:02.842 --> 01:09:03.830 line:90%
You know, I'm very old.

01:09:03.830 --> 01:09:04.819 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:09:04.819 --> 01:09:06.816 line:90%
My why is long.

01:09:06.816 --> 01:09:08.149 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: How old are you?

01:09:08.149 --> 01:09:08.840 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: 84.

01:09:08.840 --> 01:09:10.010 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: 84,
that's what I thought.

01:09:10.010 --> 01:09:10.510 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:09:10.510 --> 01:09:11.450 line:90%
1938?

01:09:11.450 --> 01:09:13.174 line:84%
In one of the
interviews, you say

01:09:13.174 --> 01:09:16.340 line:84%
that it's important to look
when you were born, 1938.

01:09:16.340 --> 01:09:23.460 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: First, so I
belong to a generation that

01:09:23.460 --> 01:09:25.140 line:90%
come exactly after the war.

01:09:25.140 --> 01:09:26.399 line:90%
I'm born in '38.

01:09:26.399 --> 01:09:29.189 line:84%
The war, the Second
World War is starting.

01:09:29.189 --> 01:09:34.649 line:84%
And I start seeing,
discovering reality

01:09:34.649 --> 01:09:39.000 line:84%
after the war in 1946, which is
the time exactly when we have

01:09:39.000 --> 01:09:42.479 line:84%
what we call the
1946 revolution.

01:09:42.479 --> 01:09:47.220 line:84%
This 1946 revolution
is the consequence

01:09:47.220 --> 01:09:50.370 line:90%
of the 1915 US occupation.

01:09:50.370 --> 01:09:53.580 line:84%
When the US occupied
Haiti, they occupied Haiti

01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:56.730 line:84%
with two things-- the
big stick in one hand,

01:09:56.730 --> 01:09:59.820 line:84%
and on the other hand,
the Jim Crow policy.

01:09:59.820 --> 01:10:03.600 line:84%
So you can imagine they
reintroduced in Haiti

01:10:03.600 --> 01:10:06.480 line:90%
the whole color problem.

01:10:06.480 --> 01:10:09.812 line:84%
This is when Price-Mars
come with the "Ainsi parla

01:10:09.812 --> 01:10:14.160 line:84%
l'oncle" on Vodou, et cetera,
and when you have the Négritude

01:10:14.160 --> 01:10:18.150 line:84%
issue being brewed in
Europe and in Haiti.

01:10:18.150 --> 01:10:23.550 line:84%
And you have Duvalier and
what we call Négrisme,

01:10:23.550 --> 01:10:28.710 line:84%
going on to answer the
opposition in the country where

01:10:28.710 --> 01:10:33.000 line:84%
the Americans were putting in
light-skinned people all over,

01:10:33.000 --> 01:10:33.960 line:90%
et cetera.

01:10:33.960 --> 01:10:35.418 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So
Jean Price-Mars,

01:10:35.418 --> 01:10:37.550 line:84%
to give some background,
was a scholar

01:10:37.550 --> 01:10:40.758 line:84%
who gave the idea that, look,
we have to go back to Africa

01:10:40.758 --> 01:10:42.300 line:84%
to understand who
we are as Haitians.

01:10:42.300 --> 01:10:43.620 line:90%
So Africa is beautiful.

01:10:43.620 --> 01:10:44.550 line:90%
Black is beautiful.

01:10:44.550 --> 01:10:47.790 line:84%
And that was in opposition
to the Americans that came in

01:10:47.790 --> 01:10:49.050 line:90%
and that were very racist.

01:10:49.050 --> 01:10:51.195 line:84%
So that's what he's
referring to as Négritude.

01:10:51.195 --> 01:10:53.070 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yeah, and
interestingly enough,

01:10:53.070 --> 01:10:58.350 line:84%
Jean Price-Mars is the
big, son of big landowners

01:10:58.350 --> 01:11:01.290 line:84%
in the countryside,
like Péralte, I mean,

01:11:01.290 --> 01:11:05.820 line:84%
because it is good not to
put everybody in one bag.

01:11:05.820 --> 01:11:09.690 line:84%
He's a "latifundista,"
a big landowner.

01:11:09.690 --> 01:11:14.790 line:84%
But he is proud of his
position, of what he is.

01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:15.930 line:90%
And he's a doctor.

01:11:15.930 --> 01:11:16.680 line:90%
He's a diplomat.

01:11:16.680 --> 01:11:17.490 line:90%
So anyway...

01:11:17.490 --> 01:11:22.332 line:84%
So in 1946, end of the
war, who was-- well,

01:11:22.332 --> 01:11:23.790 line:84%
the war ended a
little bit earlier.

01:11:23.790 --> 01:11:27.160 line:84%
Who is president is
light-skinned Élie Lescot.

01:11:27.160 --> 01:11:29.790 line:90%
And therefore, the students--

01:11:29.790 --> 01:11:32.230 line:90%
oh boy, OK.

01:11:32.230 --> 01:11:36.220 line:84%
The students made a
so-called "revolution."

01:11:36.220 --> 01:11:38.590 line:84%
And we said, Haiti
is a Black country.

01:11:38.590 --> 01:11:40.690 line:90%
The president has to be Black.

01:11:40.690 --> 01:11:42.640 line:84%
It cannot be
whatever is the idea.

01:11:42.640 --> 01:11:47.860 line:84%
But they use the term "Black,"
which is a colonial concept.

01:11:47.860 --> 01:11:52.000 line:84%
And you had as president a
Black fellow, Dumarsais Estimé,

01:11:52.000 --> 01:11:53.800 line:90%
who is a big landowner also.

01:11:53.800 --> 01:11:55.150 line:90%
You see what I mean?

01:11:55.150 --> 01:11:59.500 line:84%
So you are going to have a
possibility of development,

01:11:59.500 --> 01:12:01.390 line:84%
because that's the
epoch when the US come

01:12:01.390 --> 01:12:03.490 line:90%
with the idea of development.

01:12:03.490 --> 01:12:04.170 line:90%
Eisenhower, etc.

01:12:04.170 --> 01:12:08.140 line:84%
Truman, when
development-- so they

01:12:08.140 --> 01:12:12.310 line:84%
are going to take a whole set
of policies toward development.

01:12:12.310 --> 01:12:15.670 line:84%
We have to develop the country
to make the country capitalist,

01:12:15.670 --> 01:12:18.010 line:84%
modernize the country,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:12:18.010 --> 01:12:21.040 line:84%
So that's the period when
I am going to school.

01:12:21.040 --> 01:12:22.600 line:90%
I mean, during that period.

01:12:22.600 --> 01:12:29.170 line:84%
OK, so to-- jumping over plenty
many things, here I come.

01:12:29.170 --> 01:12:30.820 line:90%
I found a scholarship.

01:12:30.820 --> 01:12:32.620 line:90%
And I'm studying in Mexico.

01:12:32.620 --> 01:12:35.620 line:84%
So I want to know why is
it that we are so poor.

01:12:35.620 --> 01:12:39.370 line:84%
After such a wonderful economy,
a wonderful revolution,

01:12:39.370 --> 01:12:42.250 line:84%
after such a wonderful
independence, how

01:12:42.250 --> 01:12:43.780 line:90%
come we are poor?

01:12:43.780 --> 01:12:45.520 line:90%
And I start studying that.

01:12:45.520 --> 01:12:50.530 line:90%
And I wrote my license, my--

01:12:50.530 --> 01:12:51.430 line:90%
STUDENT: Thesis?

01:12:51.430 --> 01:12:52.020 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: [INAUDIBLE]?

01:12:52.020 --> 01:12:52.853 line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:12:52.853 --> 01:12:55.870 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: A
[INAUDIBLE] my first level,

01:12:55.870 --> 01:12:56.673 line:90%
my-- before master.

01:12:56.673 --> 01:12:58.090 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Bachelor's thesis.

01:12:58.090 --> 01:13:01.000 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: My bachelor's
thesis on the Republic

01:13:01.000 --> 01:13:04.180 line:84%
of Haiti, where I went
through the history trying

01:13:04.180 --> 01:13:07.990 line:84%
to explain why we are the way
we are, "poor," quote unquote,

01:13:07.990 --> 01:13:12.360 line:84%
because I am searching
for development.

01:13:12.360 --> 01:13:18.390 line:84%
At the same time, at the end of
this period, I entered the UN.

01:13:18.390 --> 01:13:20.760 line:84%
And I ended up also in
the Economic Commission

01:13:20.760 --> 01:13:24.180 line:84%
for Latin America, always
after prohibition is run,

01:13:24.180 --> 01:13:25.200 line:90%
after development.

01:13:25.200 --> 01:13:26.730 line:90%
Let's develop the country.

01:13:26.730 --> 01:13:31.890 line:84%
So I'm working on development to
find out that this is the law.

01:13:31.890 --> 01:13:34.840 line:84%
It's like the carrot you
put in front of the donkey.

01:13:34.840 --> 01:13:35.910 line:90%
I mean, you can run.

01:13:35.910 --> 01:13:37.285 line:84%
You can run, you
never get there.

01:13:37.285 --> 01:13:40.740 line:90%
[LAUGHS] et cetera, et cetera.

01:13:40.740 --> 01:13:45.660 line:84%
In the process, I
discover "culture."

01:13:45.660 --> 01:13:49.050 line:84%
I realized that Haitians
from the very beginning

01:13:49.050 --> 01:13:50.410 line:90%
have two cultures.

01:13:50.410 --> 01:13:54.810 line:84%
And I wrote my PhD thesis
with the title "The Oppressed

01:13:54.810 --> 01:13:57.150 line:90%
Culture."

01:13:57.150 --> 01:13:59.010 line:90%
I was studying in Mexico.

01:13:59.010 --> 01:14:02.595 line:84%
I was very much influenced
by their conception

01:14:02.595 --> 01:14:04.590 line:90%
of the "Creole."

01:14:04.590 --> 01:14:07.080 line:84%
So I call it "the
dominant culture."

01:14:07.080 --> 01:14:10.110 line:84%
Well, at that time,
Mexico was in the process

01:14:10.110 --> 01:14:18.240 line:84%
of "Castellanización," to teach
Spanish to the First Nations,

01:14:18.240 --> 01:14:21.360 line:84%
not because-- as far
as the ideology was,

01:14:21.360 --> 01:14:23.490 line:90%
that would help them to develop.

01:14:23.490 --> 01:14:26.550 line:84%
Now they will modernize,
speaking Spanish,

01:14:26.550 --> 01:14:28.410 line:84%
because their language
is not the language.

01:14:28.410 --> 01:14:30.210 line:84%
It's kind of dialogue
they are speaking,

01:14:30.210 --> 01:14:32.760 line:84%
things that are older
than French and Spanish.

01:14:32.760 --> 01:14:35.580 line:84%
But anyway, so
they want to erase

01:14:35.580 --> 01:14:37.480 line:90%
that to teach them to speak.

01:14:37.480 --> 01:14:47.500 line:84%
So the Creole Mexicans, they
were the dominant class.

01:14:47.500 --> 01:14:49.710 line:84%
So the dominant
culture in Haiti I

01:14:49.710 --> 01:14:52.380 line:84%
call it the "Creole
culture" as opposed

01:14:52.380 --> 01:14:58.000 line:84%
to an "oppressed culture," which
is the culture of the peasants.

01:14:58.000 --> 01:15:03.610 line:84%
And I entered in dichotomy
between "Creole" and "Bosal."

01:15:03.610 --> 01:15:07.960 line:84%
The Haitian creolized and the
Bosal just fresh from Africa.

01:15:07.960 --> 01:15:12.320 line:84%
And that gives a false
idea of the process.

01:15:12.320 --> 01:15:13.390 line:90%
But that's not the point.

01:15:13.390 --> 01:15:19.420 line:84%
That gives the basis for my
study on oppressed culture.

01:15:19.420 --> 01:15:24.370 line:84%
And I show how Africa was
erased from the history of Haiti

01:15:24.370 --> 01:15:27.640 line:84%
because of the dominance
of the Creoles.

01:15:27.640 --> 01:15:28.720 line:90%
OK, so--

01:15:28.720 --> 01:15:30.512 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Is it
"Creole" in the sense

01:15:30.512 --> 01:15:32.200 line:84%
of those who were
born in the Caribbean

01:15:32.200 --> 01:15:34.120 line:84%
and who basically
were assimilated?

01:15:34.120 --> 01:15:36.580 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Out of mixed--
with the assimilated, too, no?

01:15:36.580 --> 01:15:46.070 line:84%
And also-- well, I
proposed acculturation.

01:15:46.070 --> 01:15:48.860 line:84%
So the Creoles were those
who were acculturated,

01:15:48.860 --> 01:15:55.070 line:84%
who had learned the
Western culture who

01:15:55.070 --> 01:15:58.190 line:84%
were ruling the country, who
were the elite, et cetera,

01:15:58.190 --> 01:15:58.770 line:90%
et cetera.

01:15:58.770 --> 01:16:00.920 line:90%
You see what I mean.

01:16:00.920 --> 01:16:04.700 line:84%
While the other one was speaking
Creole [Kreyòl] the language,

01:16:04.700 --> 01:16:06.350 line:84%
and they were the
oppressed culture,

01:16:06.350 --> 01:16:08.305 line:84%
they have all sort of
other institutions,

01:16:08.305 --> 01:16:14.000 line:84%
all sort of other systems who
were "traditional, backward."

01:16:14.000 --> 01:16:15.900 line:84%
I would probably
not use "backward,"

01:16:15.900 --> 01:16:17.180 line:90%
but they were traditional.

01:16:17.180 --> 01:16:18.920 line:90%
They are not modern.

01:16:18.920 --> 01:16:23.750 line:84%
And you had to pull them
out of their traditionalism

01:16:23.750 --> 01:16:27.940 line:90%
to modernize them, more or less.

01:16:27.940 --> 01:16:34.180 line:84%
Well, that was a,
let's see, one stage.

01:16:34.180 --> 01:16:36.070 line:84%
And at the same
time, I'm discovering

01:16:36.070 --> 01:16:39.055 line:84%
that the UN has
five or six decades

01:16:39.055 --> 01:16:42.100 line:84%
of investing in
development, always

01:16:42.100 --> 01:16:43.435 line:90%
coming back to square one.

01:16:43.435 --> 01:16:45.657 line:84%
[LAUGHS] There was
no development.

01:16:45.657 --> 01:16:47.740 line:84%
But they were investing
in decades of development.

01:16:47.740 --> 01:16:50.710 line:84%
And I was working in
that, following privilege.

01:16:50.710 --> 01:16:57.130 line:84%
At the same time, I had
used-- and since the theory

01:16:57.130 --> 01:16:58.540 line:90%
of dependency--

01:16:58.540 --> 01:17:00.610 line:84%
I mean, the problem
of development

01:17:00.610 --> 01:17:02.230 line:84%
in Latin America
and the Caribbean

01:17:02.230 --> 01:17:06.250 line:84%
is that we are dependent
of the large countries,

01:17:06.250 --> 01:17:07.480 line:90%
the rich countries.

01:17:07.480 --> 01:17:11.170 line:84%
And we should try
to have a set of--

01:17:11.170 --> 01:17:14.950 line:84%
a sector of our economy that
will pull the other sector.

01:17:14.950 --> 01:17:17.100 line:84%
And this sector will
control it through

01:17:17.100 --> 01:17:18.887 line:90%
the national bourgeoisie.

01:17:18.887 --> 01:17:19.720 line:90%
You see what I mean?

01:17:19.720 --> 01:17:23.810 line:84%
This was very common in the
'60s to explain the situation.

01:17:23.810 --> 01:17:26.440 line:84%
Then you try to have an
independent sector who

01:17:26.440 --> 01:17:34.120 line:84%
will be the locomotive to
pull the train, et cetera,

01:17:34.120 --> 01:17:34.630 line:90%
et cetera.

01:17:34.630 --> 01:17:36.250 line:84%
But obviously
nobody never had it.

01:17:36.250 --> 01:17:38.710 line:84%
Or those who had
it were co-opted.

01:17:38.710 --> 01:17:42.370 line:90%
But this is what we were using.

01:17:42.370 --> 01:17:46.270 line:84%
But I still was
quite dissatisfied,

01:17:46.270 --> 01:17:50.380 line:84%
because that will not
take Haiti out of poverty.

01:17:50.380 --> 01:17:56.830 line:84%
And not only that, that
implies Duvalier as a dictator,

01:17:56.830 --> 01:18:00.790 line:84%
the son of Duvalier, et cetera,
et cetera, an effort by the US

01:18:00.790 --> 01:18:07.990 line:84%
to industrialize the country,
et cetera, et cetera, all coming

01:18:07.990 --> 01:18:11.680 line:90%
always back to this poverty.

01:18:11.680 --> 01:18:13.870 line:90%
So this is when I start--

01:18:13.870 --> 01:18:16.900 line:84%
first of all, I
move out of the idea

01:18:16.900 --> 01:18:19.690 line:84%
of "development," realizing
that "development" is not

01:18:19.690 --> 01:18:21.880 line:90%
bringing us anywhere.

01:18:24.610 --> 01:18:28.240 line:90%
And therefore, what?

01:18:28.240 --> 01:18:32.470 line:84%
In the process of studying
the oppressed culture,

01:18:32.470 --> 01:18:36.470 line:84%
I found the
counter-plantation system.

01:18:36.470 --> 01:18:38.860 line:90%
I found the village society.

01:18:38.860 --> 01:18:42.130 line:84%
And I was, by that time,
working in Trinidad

01:18:42.130 --> 01:18:43.870 line:90%
with my Caribbean friends.

01:18:43.870 --> 01:18:46.930 line:84%
I started realizing
that plantation--

01:18:46.930 --> 01:18:48.640 line:84%
because we are
talking development--

01:18:48.640 --> 01:18:54.505 line:84%
is the locomotive, no,
the key part of the train.

01:18:54.505 --> 01:18:55.630 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: The engine?

01:18:55.630 --> 01:18:57.670 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: The
engine of development,

01:18:57.670 --> 01:18:58.630 line:90%
et cetera, et cetera.

01:18:58.630 --> 01:19:04.380 line:84%
And I accept with them
that the bush society is

01:19:04.380 --> 01:19:08.780 line:90%
a ...metayage... land tenure?

01:19:08.780 --> 01:19:10.740 line:90%
Not land tenure... metayage?

01:19:15.710 --> 01:19:18.200 line:90%
You say that in Saint Lucia.

01:19:18.200 --> 01:19:18.830 line:90%
Oh, boy--

01:19:18.830 --> 01:19:20.480 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: I was about
to say that, actually.

01:19:20.480 --> 01:19:21.230 line:90%
Let me look it up.

01:19:23.710 --> 01:19:27.100 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: When you
rent the land to somebody,

01:19:27.100 --> 01:19:28.480 line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH].

01:19:28.480 --> 01:19:31.630 line:84%
STUDENT: Oh, I have
an Arabic word for it.

01:19:31.630 --> 01:19:32.800 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:19:32.800 --> 01:19:35.800 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Anyway,
so I start thinking

01:19:35.800 --> 01:19:42.010 line:84%
of this form of production,
sharecropping, where

01:19:42.010 --> 01:19:47.170 line:84%
traditional, because you
should go into a plantation,

01:19:47.170 --> 01:19:50.140 line:84%
into a trade union,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:19:50.140 --> 01:19:52.690 line:84%
I must tell you also that
the same time with Cuba

01:19:52.690 --> 01:19:54.160 line:90%
is making is revolution.

01:19:54.160 --> 01:19:56.020 line:90%
Cuba is nationalizing.

01:19:56.020 --> 01:19:57.670 line:90%
Cuba is moving forward.

01:19:57.670 --> 01:20:00.237 line:84%
And we were all
obviously pro-Cuban,

01:20:00.237 --> 01:20:01.570 line:90%
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

01:20:01.570 --> 01:20:03.710 line:90%
So we are seeing that.

01:20:03.710 --> 01:20:06.250 line:84%
But this is not
working in Haiti.

01:20:06.250 --> 01:20:09.280 line:84%
I mean, this is not work--
not only this is not working,

01:20:09.280 --> 01:20:13.390 line:84%
the Cubans in their
efforts are helping Angola.

01:20:13.390 --> 01:20:16.690 line:90%
They are helping Bolivia.

01:20:16.690 --> 01:20:22.060 line:84%
Haiti is just around the corner,
[LAUGHS] et cetera, et cetera.

01:20:22.060 --> 01:20:25.570 line:84%
So people start to
have doubts, no?

01:20:25.570 --> 01:20:29.740 line:84%
And I start seeing that the
economic development is not

01:20:29.740 --> 01:20:33.190 line:84%
giving us a result. And we have
to think in terms of culture.

01:20:33.190 --> 01:20:36.850 line:84%
And I start moving with the
counter-plantation system,

01:20:36.850 --> 01:20:38.860 line:90%
looking at it at its importance.

01:20:38.860 --> 01:20:43.300 line:84%
And I start discovering the
need for working with Kreyòl,

01:20:43.300 --> 01:20:49.540 line:84%
for participation of the people
in the development system.

01:20:49.540 --> 01:20:55.240 line:84%
OK, so reaching that point,
I wrote a series of article

01:20:55.240 --> 01:21:00.580 line:84%
and publish a book called
"Haiti and Its Elites..."--

01:21:00.580 --> 01:21:01.810 line:90%
no?

01:21:01.810 --> 01:21:03.100 line:90%
"...Dialogue of Death."

01:21:03.100 --> 01:21:04.090 line:90%
Of "deaf"?

01:21:04.090 --> 01:21:05.620 line:90%
People who can't hear.

01:21:05.620 --> 01:21:06.340 line:90%
STUDENT: Deaf.

01:21:06.340 --> 01:21:07.240 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Deaf?

01:21:07.240 --> 01:21:09.040 line:90%
OK, no?

01:21:09.040 --> 01:21:16.940 line:84%
And this is the-- let's see,
the book announcing Haiti

01:21:16.940 --> 01:21:21.230 line:84%
and the decolonial history,
because it is at that time that

01:21:21.230 --> 01:21:27.200 line:84%
I met my friends of the colonial
thinking who thought that

01:21:27.200 --> 01:21:31.810 line:84%
my book on oppressed
culture, in fact,

01:21:31.810 --> 01:21:35.290 line:84%
announces the whole
decolonial thinking,

01:21:35.290 --> 01:21:37.060 line:90%
because I was trying to see--

01:21:37.060 --> 01:21:39.460 line:84%
and this is the question
I was asking myself,

01:21:39.460 --> 01:21:43.810 line:84%
since my book on oppressed
culture is my PhD thesis.

01:21:43.810 --> 01:21:48.460 line:84%
So my PhD thesis is saying,
is asking, what is it

01:21:48.460 --> 01:21:50.505 line:90%
that we have done for ourselves?

01:21:50.505 --> 01:21:52.630 line:84%
I mean, plantation, planting,
all sorts of things--

01:21:52.630 --> 01:21:54.760 line:84%
but we have nothing
to do in plantation.

01:21:54.760 --> 01:21:56.500 line:90%
That was imposed on us, no?

01:21:56.500 --> 01:21:58.400 line:84%
I didn't raise the
question like that.

01:21:58.400 --> 01:22:01.000 line:84%
But I could have
done it in that book,

01:22:01.000 --> 01:22:08.660 line:84%
saying that a plantation is
not a Caribbean institution.

01:22:08.660 --> 01:22:12.850 line:84%
It is the time when Caribbean
is called "plantation America."

01:22:12.850 --> 01:22:15.430 line:84%
I said, no, no, we
have something else.

01:22:15.430 --> 01:22:18.972 line:84%
Caribbean cannot be English
Caribbean, Dutch Caribbean,

01:22:18.972 --> 01:22:19.680 line:90%
French Caribbean.

01:22:19.680 --> 01:22:21.650 line:90%
Caribbean is Caribbean, period.

01:22:21.650 --> 01:22:23.960 line:84%
I mean, there is
something we have done,

01:22:23.960 --> 01:22:26.680 line:84%
which is not English,
French, British, or Dutch,

01:22:26.680 --> 01:22:29.770 line:90%
what have you, or Spanish.

01:22:29.770 --> 01:22:33.520 line:84%
So I start looking for
what we have learned.

01:22:33.520 --> 01:22:37.080 line:84%
This is what I call
"a dialogue of deaf,"

01:22:37.080 --> 01:22:44.100 line:84%
because the elite is French,
the elite speak French,

01:22:44.100 --> 01:22:46.500 line:84%
the laws are French, et
cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

01:22:46.500 --> 01:22:52.110 line:84%
And in one discussion in Aquin,
I was presenting the book.

01:22:52.110 --> 01:22:54.270 line:84%
And somebody said,
but what you're saying

01:22:54.270 --> 01:22:55.350 line:90%
there is not an elite.

01:22:55.350 --> 01:22:57.600 line:90%
It is an oligarchy.

01:22:57.600 --> 01:23:01.050 line:84%
I say, oh God, I should
have thought of that,

01:23:01.050 --> 01:23:02.740 line:90%
because elite is very simple.

01:23:02.740 --> 01:23:05.790 line:84%
If we are 10
shoemakers, the best

01:23:05.790 --> 01:23:07.710 line:90%
are the elite of the shoemakers.

01:23:07.710 --> 01:23:11.820 line:84%
But if the best does not
behave as giving best shoes,

01:23:11.820 --> 01:23:14.610 line:84%
they will go down
among the others.

01:23:14.610 --> 01:23:17.250 line:84%
And other people
will come as elite.

01:23:17.250 --> 01:23:19.380 line:84%
This is not the case
in our societies.

01:23:19.380 --> 01:23:21.090 line:84%
These elite of French
speaking people

01:23:21.090 --> 01:23:27.420 line:84%
never go down, because
they are an oligarchy.

01:23:27.420 --> 01:23:29.670 line:84%
So I realized that we
do not have an elite.

01:23:29.670 --> 01:23:32.100 line:84%
We have an elite, the
Vodou priests, the manbo.

01:23:32.100 --> 01:23:34.410 line:90%
And those are elites.

01:23:34.410 --> 01:23:37.050 line:84%
But those who govern the
country-- our French speakers--

01:23:37.050 --> 01:23:38.313 line:90%
are not necessarily elite.

01:23:38.313 --> 01:23:39.480 line:90%
In fact, they are not elite.

01:23:39.480 --> 01:23:42.450 line:84%
They are those who
are holding us down.

01:23:42.450 --> 01:23:46.020 line:84%
And therefore, I
start now discovering

01:23:46.020 --> 01:23:49.260 line:90%
the decolonial thought.

01:23:49.260 --> 01:23:52.290 line:84%
Let me study what we have
done, how we have done that.

01:23:52.290 --> 01:23:55.260 line:84%
How did the Haitian
came to be Haitian?

01:23:55.260 --> 01:23:58.230 line:84%
And what makes a Haitian
different from a Jamaican,

01:23:58.230 --> 01:24:01.230 line:84%
from a Vincentian, or
from a Trinidadian?

01:24:01.230 --> 01:24:04.230 line:84%
Because their local
history's different.

01:24:04.230 --> 01:24:05.890 line:84%
You see, when you
take-- for instance,

01:24:05.890 --> 01:24:08.310 line:84%
I had the good fortune through
the Economic Commission

01:24:08.310 --> 01:24:10.140 line:90%
to work 10 years in Trinidad.

01:24:10.140 --> 01:24:13.050 line:84%
And I tell the Trinidadians
that you never knew slavery.

01:24:13.050 --> 01:24:14.830 line:90%
You were a happy people.

01:24:14.830 --> 01:24:16.620 line:84%
And when you think
of it, that's true.

01:24:16.620 --> 01:24:21.060 line:84%
The Trinidadians, Trinidad was
Spaniard, Spanish, Spaniard,

01:24:21.060 --> 01:24:22.440 line:90%
no?

01:24:22.440 --> 01:24:30.300 line:84%
When Haiti started its
revolution beginning of 1790s,

01:24:30.300 --> 01:24:33.390 line:84%
Spain starts receiving
the planters running away

01:24:33.390 --> 01:24:35.940 line:84%
from Saint-Domingue
to such an extent

01:24:35.940 --> 01:24:38.310 line:84%
that Trinidadians speak the
same Creole that we speak.

01:24:38.310 --> 01:24:41.100 line:84%
And we understand each
other, because they will come

01:24:41.100 --> 01:24:44.070 line:90%
to Trinidad with their slaves.

01:24:44.070 --> 01:24:46.740 line:84%
And there are several
examples like that.

01:24:46.740 --> 01:24:52.080 line:84%
And then the Spaniards
and the French planters,

01:24:52.080 --> 01:24:55.830 line:84%
which Trinidadians called French
Creoles, the French planter

01:24:55.830 --> 01:24:57.750 line:84%
and the slave will
establish Creole

01:24:57.750 --> 01:25:02.040 line:84%
as the basic language of the
land together with Spanish,

01:25:02.040 --> 01:25:06.540 line:84%
in certain pockets speak
Spanish, French, [? but-- ?]

01:25:06.540 --> 01:25:10.200 line:84%
so the Spanish and Creole,
above all, Spanish and Creole.

01:25:10.200 --> 01:25:14.850 line:90%
So soon after, the British--

01:25:14.850 --> 01:25:18.540 line:84%
Spain cedes the
island to the British.

01:25:18.540 --> 01:25:22.470 line:84%
And in the ceded island, the
British has to recognize,

01:25:22.470 --> 01:25:25.870 line:84%
it acknowledges the
internal laws of this land.

01:25:25.870 --> 01:25:27.900 line:90%
So the British were governing.

01:25:27.900 --> 01:25:31.410 line:84%
And the Trinidadians were
speaking Creole and French.

01:25:31.410 --> 01:25:34.080 line:90%
The British was the owner.

01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:40.110 line:84%
So I realized at that
point in time, by 1817,

01:25:40.110 --> 01:25:45.510 line:84%
we are 1803, 1804
when the French start

01:25:45.510 --> 01:25:47.100 line:90%
flowing in the country.

01:25:47.100 --> 01:25:50.100 line:84%
By 10 years or 20 years
after, the British

01:25:50.100 --> 01:25:52.140 line:90%
stopped the slave trade.

01:25:52.140 --> 01:25:57.355 line:84%
And every time they catch a
slave boat in the Atlantic,

01:25:57.355 --> 01:25:58.980 line:84%
they come with the
slaves and drop them

01:25:58.980 --> 01:26:01.020 line:84%
in-- the so-called
slave captives,

01:26:01.020 --> 01:26:02.680 line:90%
dropped them in Trinidad.

01:26:02.680 --> 01:26:06.000 line:84%
So the population of
Blacks in Trinidad

01:26:06.000 --> 01:26:09.270 line:84%
are created without
slave trades.

01:26:09.270 --> 01:26:13.920 line:84%
So the slave masters had
to take care of the slaves,

01:26:13.920 --> 01:26:17.160 line:84%
because they cannot produce
and buy all the slaves.

01:26:17.160 --> 01:26:20.220 line:84%
They must make sure that they
reproduce themselves locally.

01:26:20.220 --> 01:26:27.517 line:84%
Not only that, soon after
1838, the British stop slavery.

01:26:27.517 --> 01:26:28.350 line:90%
You see what I mean?

01:26:28.350 --> 01:26:31.200 line:84%
So the slavery
Haitians have known

01:26:31.200 --> 01:26:34.740 line:84%
for 100 centuries
with a slave trade

01:26:34.740 --> 01:26:37.290 line:84%
is not what the
Trinidadian has known, no?

01:26:37.290 --> 01:26:38.730 line:84%
And when you go
to Trinidad, they

01:26:38.730 --> 01:26:41.520 line:84%
are happy, happy going,
happy looking people.

01:26:41.520 --> 01:26:42.930 line:90%
They have never have problems.

01:26:42.930 --> 01:26:43.710 line:90%
They never fight.

01:26:43.710 --> 01:26:45.300 line:84%
They do not even
know how to fight,

01:26:45.300 --> 01:26:48.520 line:90%
because they never had to fight.

01:26:48.520 --> 01:26:50.670 line:84%
From the very
beginning, the masters

01:26:50.670 --> 01:26:54.360 line:84%
has to make sure that they
have a slave for tomorrow

01:26:54.360 --> 01:26:55.170 line:90%
at the beginning.

01:26:55.170 --> 01:26:59.190 line:90%
Then slavery become abolished.

01:26:59.190 --> 01:27:01.680 line:84%
So I tell them, you don't
know what slavery is.

01:27:01.680 --> 01:27:02.700 line:90%
You can imagine that.

01:27:02.700 --> 01:27:07.920 line:84%
Not only that, they start with
the indentured and flooding

01:27:07.920 --> 01:27:11.790 line:84%
the country with indentured,
so a whole other dynamic

01:27:11.790 --> 01:27:15.075 line:84%
and a-- whole
different sets of--

01:27:15.075 --> 01:27:18.540 line:84%
OK, this experience
in Trinidad now

01:27:18.540 --> 01:27:21.210 line:84%
then come and give
me the possibility

01:27:21.210 --> 01:27:25.260 line:84%
of seeing local
history developing

01:27:25.260 --> 01:27:29.520 line:84%
and how we are in
our local history

01:27:29.520 --> 01:27:34.680 line:84%
producing a way out of
quote unquote "our misery."

01:27:34.680 --> 01:27:38.340 line:84%
This is how I went and see the
importance of the language,

01:27:38.340 --> 01:27:41.640 line:84%
the importance of the community,
the importance of the higglers

01:27:41.640 --> 01:27:44.400 line:84%
because [INAUDIBLE],,
and I must say also,

01:27:44.400 --> 01:27:45.910 line:90%
the importance of the yard.

01:27:45.910 --> 01:27:48.370 line:84%
I have some strong
friends, Jamaican friends,

01:27:48.370 --> 01:27:53.700 line:84%
particularly Erna Brodber,
who wrote on yards.

01:27:53.700 --> 01:27:59.900 line:84%
That is-- and the way the
yard is important in Jamaica--

01:27:59.900 --> 01:28:01.430 line:90%
in Kingston she wrote that.

01:28:01.430 --> 01:28:03.600 line:84%
Me, I'm seeing the world
in the countryside.

01:28:03.600 --> 01:28:05.150 line:90%
Then you go to Dominica.

01:28:05.150 --> 01:28:07.370 line:84%
There are several
studies in Dominica

01:28:07.370 --> 01:28:10.760 line:84%
and Saint Lucia on the
family property system.

01:28:10.760 --> 01:28:14.000 line:84%
All of those are institutions
that are Caribbean.

01:28:14.000 --> 01:28:15.890 line:84%
It's not that you don't
have family property

01:28:15.890 --> 01:28:16.850 line:90%
system in France.

01:28:16.850 --> 01:28:20.240 line:84%
But it doesn't characterize
the land tenure system,

01:28:20.240 --> 01:28:23.300 line:84%
while in other countries
it is the basis

01:28:23.300 --> 01:28:24.740 line:90%
of land-tenure system.

01:28:24.740 --> 01:28:27.860 line:84%
And the work I have
used in Dominica

01:28:27.860 --> 01:28:31.910 line:84%
shows that those who put-- who
has land in the land tenure

01:28:31.910 --> 01:28:33.890 line:84%
system produce more
than those who have land

01:28:33.890 --> 01:28:35.360 line:90%
in the et cetera, et cetera.

01:28:35.360 --> 01:28:39.710 line:84%
So this is how I
end up in my why.

01:28:39.710 --> 01:28:43.010 line:84%
That's my why, because
I reach a point where

01:28:43.010 --> 01:28:47.390 line:84%
I can explain to myself
what they call poverty

01:28:47.390 --> 01:28:50.000 line:84%
and why we are quite
happy in that poverty,

01:28:50.000 --> 01:28:51.890 line:90%
if you want to see it that way.

01:28:51.890 --> 01:28:56.420 line:84%
We will be unhappy in the
city, because in the city,

01:28:56.420 --> 01:28:59.930 line:84%
we are not able to reproduce
the yard, properly,

01:28:59.930 --> 01:29:04.090 line:84%
to have our resources
to reproduce it.

01:29:04.090 --> 01:29:06.070 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: OK,
that's a good question.

01:29:06.070 --> 01:29:06.668 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:29:06.668 --> 01:29:07.960 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: My why, [LAUGHS].

01:29:07.960 --> 01:29:09.370 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Yeah.
that's a good one.

01:29:09.370 --> 01:29:10.600 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: And
I had to answer it.

01:29:10.600 --> 01:29:11.023 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

01:29:11.023 --> 01:29:12.356 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Yes, you had to.

01:29:12.356 --> 01:29:14.660 line:90%
And you gave a beautiful answer.

01:29:14.660 --> 01:29:16.570 line:90%
So Jezhara?

01:29:16.570 --> 01:29:18.190 line:84%
STUDENT: So I guess
my question is--

01:29:18.190 --> 01:29:19.482 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Remove your mask.

01:29:19.482 --> 01:29:21.610 line:84%
STUDENT: Oh, so
Creole languages are

01:29:21.610 --> 01:29:24.580 line:84%
very important to the
Caribbean community.

01:29:24.580 --> 01:29:28.000 line:84%
But we refer to English
Creole as broken English.

01:29:28.000 --> 01:29:31.870 line:84%
Or you promote French
over Haitian Creole.

01:29:31.870 --> 01:29:34.060 line:84%
So my question is,
what do you think

01:29:34.060 --> 01:29:35.620 line:84%
will happen to our
communities if we

01:29:35.620 --> 01:29:38.200 line:90%
keep rejecting that Creole?

01:29:38.200 --> 01:29:39.640 line:90%
STUDENT: I love this question.

01:29:39.640 --> 01:29:41.165 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: If you keep
projecting our Creole?

01:29:41.165 --> 01:29:42.020 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Rejecting, rejecting--

01:29:42.020 --> 01:29:44.830 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Rejecting,
oh, it's that's suicidal.

01:29:44.830 --> 01:29:46.540 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: If
the state apparatus--

01:29:46.540 --> 01:29:49.420 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: But
first of all, let

01:29:49.420 --> 01:29:52.750 line:84%
me say that I have
an unproven theory.

01:29:52.750 --> 01:29:56.440 line:84%
And I'm trying to convince
some linguists to work on it

01:29:56.440 --> 01:29:57.220 line:90%
because I cannot.

01:29:57.220 --> 01:30:00.820 line:84%
But they do "la sourde oreille,"
they don't pay attention.

01:30:00.820 --> 01:30:03.370 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:30:03.370 --> 01:30:07.000 line:84%
Forget about the English
Creole and French Creole,

01:30:07.000 --> 01:30:09.200 line:90%
because they are identified.

01:30:09.200 --> 01:30:10.150 line:90%
They exist.

01:30:10.150 --> 01:30:11.050 line:90%
They are bodies.

01:30:11.050 --> 01:30:13.405 line:84%
They have their syntaxes,
they have their vocabulary.

01:30:13.405 --> 01:30:14.560 line:90%
They have their words.

01:30:14.560 --> 01:30:16.090 line:90%
For instance, I don't go.

01:30:16.090 --> 01:30:16.895 line:90%
Mick, I see you.

01:30:16.895 --> 01:30:17.770 line:90%
You know what I mean?

01:30:17.770 --> 01:30:18.760 line:90%
These exist.

01:30:18.760 --> 01:30:20.410 line:90%
They are there to see.

01:30:20.410 --> 01:30:23.920 line:84%
My problems start when
I speak with the Puerto

01:30:23.920 --> 01:30:25.510 line:90%
Ricans and the Dominicans.

01:30:25.510 --> 01:30:26.425 line:90%
They speak Spanish.

01:30:26.425 --> 01:30:28.090 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:30:28.090 --> 01:30:31.360 line:90%
They speak an imperial language.

01:30:31.360 --> 01:30:35.000 line:84%
Yet I'm pretty sure that this
imperial language carries

01:30:35.000 --> 01:30:39.820 line:84%
a historical tradition that
departs from the Castilian--

01:30:39.820 --> 01:30:43.247 line:84%
native Spanish from the
Castilian tradition,

01:30:43.247 --> 01:30:44.080 line:90%
you see what I mean?

01:30:44.080 --> 01:30:45.940 line:84%
And they must have
concept like we

01:30:45.940 --> 01:30:48.700 line:84%
have "whites,"
which is not white,

01:30:48.700 --> 01:30:51.520 line:90%
like "black" which is not black.

01:30:51.520 --> 01:30:54.880 line:84%
And this is what I call
a linguistic archaeology.

01:30:54.880 --> 01:30:57.520 line:84%
I want somebody to
tell me what the Puerto

01:30:57.520 --> 01:31:00.550 line:84%
Rican or the
Dominican or the Cuban

01:31:00.550 --> 01:31:05.320 line:84%
are producing that
define them and make them

01:31:05.320 --> 01:31:08.770 line:90%
different from the imperial--

01:31:08.770 --> 01:31:09.755 line:90%
STUDENT: Castilian--

01:31:09.755 --> 01:31:11.380 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Going
from the Castilian.

01:31:11.380 --> 01:31:12.220 line:90%
You see what I mean?

01:31:12.220 --> 01:31:18.310 line:84%
OK, now in our case, the
problem, basic problem--

01:31:18.310 --> 01:31:23.980 line:84%
and this is a problem with
France and the French UNESCO

01:31:23.980 --> 01:31:29.350 line:84%
and so on, they have to
justify their existence.

01:31:29.350 --> 01:31:33.760 line:84%
They come with the
idea of "universality,"

01:31:33.760 --> 01:31:37.840 line:84%
the "universal" civilization,
"universal" God knows what.

01:31:37.840 --> 01:31:41.890 line:84%
And they want to
destroy all diversity.

01:31:41.890 --> 01:31:47.530 line:84%
And our elite buy into
that "universal" thing.

01:31:47.530 --> 01:31:49.790 line:84%
And they will tell
you, for instance,

01:31:49.790 --> 01:31:53.140 line:84%
if the Haitians speak Kreyòl,
they will be isolated.

01:31:53.140 --> 01:31:54.910 line:90%
I say, let's go quiet.

01:31:54.910 --> 01:31:58.360 line:90%
Haiti has now 8 to 12 million.

01:31:58.360 --> 01:32:01.647 line:84%
When I visited Curaçao,
they had 90 million, 90--

01:32:01.647 --> 01:32:02.980 line:90%
yeah, "quatre-vingt dix mille"--

01:32:02.980 --> 01:32:04.042 line:90%
90,000.

01:32:04.042 --> 01:32:05.000 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: 90,000.

01:32:05.000 --> 01:32:05.875 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: 90,000.

01:32:05.875 --> 01:32:07.538 line:84%
One day, I was
living in Trinidad.

01:32:07.538 --> 01:32:09.580 line:84%
And I wanted-- you know,
Trinidad is very strict,

01:32:09.580 --> 01:32:10.330 line:90%
quite British.

01:32:10.330 --> 01:32:11.740 line:90%
And you cannot buy everything.

01:32:11.740 --> 01:32:13.240 line:90%
IBM, everything is controlled.

01:32:13.240 --> 01:32:15.760 line:84%
So I'm going to Curaçao,
which is a commercial place.

01:32:15.760 --> 01:32:18.310 line:84%
I have to buy
something from IBM.

01:32:18.310 --> 01:32:20.230 line:90%
But I don't know where IBM is.

01:32:20.230 --> 01:32:21.850 line:90%
So I come on the street.

01:32:21.850 --> 01:32:23.140 line:90%
I saw a fellow well dressed.

01:32:23.140 --> 01:32:23.800 line:90%
He should know.

01:32:23.800 --> 01:32:24.722 line:90%
I ask, where is IBM?

01:32:24.722 --> 01:32:25.930 line:90%
He say, no, no, I don't know.

01:32:25.930 --> 01:32:26.890 line:90%
Ask that lady.

01:32:26.890 --> 01:32:29.200 line:90%
And the lady is selling fruits.

01:32:29.200 --> 01:32:34.350 line:84%
So I tell the lady, sorry,
excuse me, where is IBM?

01:32:34.350 --> 01:32:36.100 line:84%
In what language you
want the information?

01:32:36.100 --> 01:32:39.280 line:84%
[LAUGHS] The [INAUDIBLE]
from Curaçao,

01:32:39.280 --> 01:32:41.440 line:90%
she speaks to us a Papiamento.

01:32:41.440 --> 01:32:42.358 line:90%
But she speaks Dutch.

01:32:42.358 --> 01:32:43.150 line:90%
She speaks English.

01:32:43.150 --> 01:32:43.840 line:90%
She speaks French.

01:32:43.840 --> 01:32:45.215 line:84%
She speaks all
sorts of language.

01:32:45.215 --> 01:32:49.010 line:84%
I could choose the language
to receive my information.

01:32:49.010 --> 01:32:51.880 line:84%
Because in Curaçao,
as you would say,

01:32:51.880 --> 01:32:53.500 line:90%
Papiamento is taught by school.

01:32:53.500 --> 01:32:56.960 line:84%
They teach Papiamento
in the schools.

01:32:56.960 --> 01:32:59.710 line:84%
And the people speaking
Papiamento can speak--

01:32:59.710 --> 01:33:02.800 line:84%
learn and speak, because of
the commercial, any language

01:33:02.800 --> 01:33:03.880 line:90%
you want.

01:33:03.880 --> 01:33:06.610 line:84%
I mean, they are not
isolated by Papiamento.

01:33:06.610 --> 01:33:12.130 line:84%
Papiamento helps them to
be linked with the others.

01:33:12.130 --> 01:33:18.160 line:84%
So if we manage and control
our local languages, no?--

01:33:18.160 --> 01:33:22.360 line:84%
Jamaican, Haitians, Vincentians,
et cetera, et cetera, and we

01:33:22.360 --> 01:33:26.770 line:84%
can find out what is the
specificity of our language

01:33:26.770 --> 01:33:29.950 line:84%
together with other, we learn
to appreciate our language,

01:33:29.950 --> 01:33:33.310 line:84%
to appreciate their language,
and to appreciate us,

01:33:33.310 --> 01:33:38.230 line:84%
who have invented a way
of respecting everybody.

01:33:38.230 --> 01:33:40.300 line:84%
So to my mind,
this is the point.

01:33:40.300 --> 01:33:43.820 line:90%
And this is not a theory.

01:33:43.820 --> 01:33:45.530 line:90%
But I don't know how to call it.

01:33:45.530 --> 01:33:48.170 line:84%
They are when they
are putting down.

01:33:48.170 --> 01:33:50.480 line:84%
Or if Haiti speaks Kreyòl,
they will be isolated.

01:33:50.480 --> 01:33:53.000 line:90%
Aren't we isolated now?

01:33:53.000 --> 01:33:56.000 line:84%
And since when do
we speak French?

01:33:56.000 --> 01:33:59.420 line:84%
If France just learn
how to speak French--

01:33:59.420 --> 01:34:00.320 line:90%
they just learn.

01:34:00.320 --> 01:34:01.700 line:90%
It's 100 year.

01:34:01.700 --> 01:34:03.860 line:90%
I mean, French is---

01:34:03.860 --> 01:34:06.650 line:84%
I don't know, English
is worse, they told me.

01:34:06.650 --> 01:34:09.740 line:84%
And French is the language
of the administration

01:34:09.740 --> 01:34:10.730 line:90%
and of Paris.

01:34:10.730 --> 01:34:14.930 line:84%
And French was imposed on
the provinces of France

01:34:14.930 --> 01:34:18.950 line:90%
to strengthen the central power.

01:34:18.950 --> 01:34:21.630 line:90%
And it remains.

01:34:21.630 --> 01:34:30.110 line:84%
I mean, Eugen Weber
present that in his book,

01:34:30.110 --> 01:34:32.190 line:90%
"Le parler de terroirs"--.

01:34:32.190 --> 01:34:36.110 line:84%
It's really important,
because French is not

01:34:36.110 --> 01:34:39.650 line:84%
a country that come
from all French people,

01:34:39.650 --> 01:34:42.680 line:84%
because French was a
country of peasants.

01:34:42.680 --> 01:34:51.650 line:84%
And the plantation is the first
capitalist, rational economic

01:34:51.650 --> 01:34:52.520 line:90%
concern.

01:34:52.520 --> 01:34:55.167 line:84%
Now this is why when you
have English in Jamaica,

01:34:55.167 --> 01:34:57.500 line:84%
you have Creole English,
Jamaicans speak Creole English,

01:34:57.500 --> 01:35:01.220 line:84%
period, all Jamaicans, while in
France, you leave one village,

01:35:01.220 --> 01:35:03.710 line:84%
you go to another, and you
cannot understand what they are

01:35:03.710 --> 01:35:07.550 line:84%
saying, because they have
a small microclimate,

01:35:07.550 --> 01:35:12.200 line:84%
microvillage without this
connection that would give you

01:35:12.200 --> 01:35:15.050 line:90%
an internal comprehension.

01:35:15.050 --> 01:35:16.040 line:90%
This is a fact.

01:35:16.040 --> 01:35:18.560 line:90%
And when they say Haiti--

01:35:18.560 --> 01:35:21.960 line:84%
and then, well, let's
say Haiti speaks French.

01:35:21.960 --> 01:35:23.760 line:90%
From colonial times up to now.

01:35:23.760 --> 01:35:26.580 line:90%
Please, please.

01:35:26.580 --> 01:35:32.010 line:84%
[LAUGHS] Now it's-- they find
that insolent and insulting

01:35:32.010 --> 01:35:35.580 line:84%
that in some Canadian
and French University,

01:35:35.580 --> 01:35:38.760 line:84%
they request the Haitian
to present a kind of TOEFL

01:35:38.760 --> 01:35:42.270 line:84%
examination to prove
that this French.

01:35:42.270 --> 01:35:43.830 line:90%
And this is the fact.

01:35:43.830 --> 01:35:45.210 line:90%
I teach at university.

01:35:45.210 --> 01:35:50.190 line:84%
And there is university student
who really cannot express--

01:35:50.190 --> 01:35:52.440 line:84%
cannot present an
essay in proper French.

01:35:52.440 --> 01:35:53.868 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Yep, yep.

01:35:53.868 --> 01:35:55.410 line:84%
STUDENT: It's also
interesting on top

01:35:55.410 --> 01:36:01.230 line:84%
of that about how they keep
that structure in French Canada,

01:36:01.230 --> 01:36:05.430 line:84%
despite even French Canadians
being treated as lesser

01:36:05.430 --> 01:36:11.310 line:84%
in the French eyes, because they
call Canadian French disgusting

01:36:11.310 --> 01:36:13.380 line:84%
French, like they
call it lesser French.

01:36:13.380 --> 01:36:16.150 line:84%
So it's funny how
even in Canada,

01:36:16.150 --> 01:36:18.600 line:84%
where it's like their
French is deemed lesser,

01:36:18.600 --> 01:36:19.950 line:90%
they still keep [INAUDIBLE].

01:36:19.950 --> 01:36:22.242 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Yeah, because
they have the English also,

01:36:22.242 --> 01:36:24.930 line:84%
because they have the
English they think is better.

01:36:24.930 --> 01:36:26.788 line:90%
I mean--

01:36:26.788 --> 01:36:29.080 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: So this is
really, really, really good.

01:36:29.080 --> 01:36:31.590 line:84%
So we've had an amazing
set of questions.

01:36:31.590 --> 01:36:34.850 line:84%
And I wanted to follow
up on Jazhara's question.

01:36:34.850 --> 01:36:38.840 line:84%
And here I want to go
back to the UNESCO issue,

01:36:38.840 --> 01:36:41.870 line:84%
because we heard this
very eloquent plea

01:36:41.870 --> 01:36:46.040 line:84%
from Ambassadeur Dominique
Dupuy asking UNESCO

01:36:46.040 --> 01:36:48.770 line:84%
on what grounds are
you classifying Creole

01:36:48.770 --> 01:36:50.138 line:90%
languages as lesser languages?

01:36:50.138 --> 01:36:50.930 line:90%
They are languages.

01:36:50.930 --> 01:36:52.310 line:90%
That's it.

01:36:52.310 --> 01:36:55.100 line:84%
But your work-- and one thing
which I love about your work

01:36:55.100 --> 01:36:58.850 line:84%
is that you show the beauty
and power of Haitian Creole.

01:36:58.850 --> 01:37:02.450 line:84%
You show the richness
of the vocabulary

01:37:02.450 --> 01:37:05.180 line:84%
and how the vocabulary can tell
us so much about our history.

01:37:05.180 --> 01:37:08.473 line:84%
And the fact that you put out
so beautifully words like "ras,"

01:37:08.473 --> 01:37:10.640 line:84%
"abitan," "moun," "nèg,"
"blan," "blanch," "milat"--

01:37:16.570 --> 01:37:20.230 line:84%
that all these words,
for them to have evolved

01:37:20.230 --> 01:37:22.070 line:84%
that semantics--
in fact, that's yet

01:37:22.070 --> 01:37:23.870 line:84%
another argument
against the idea

01:37:23.870 --> 01:37:25.700 line:84%
that Creoles
evolved from Pidgin,

01:37:25.700 --> 01:37:29.600 line:84%
because you see if Pidgin's
supposed to be like a tabula

01:37:29.600 --> 01:37:33.950 line:84%
rasa, like an empty
void of language

01:37:33.950 --> 01:37:35.948 line:84%
from which you create
a new language.

01:37:35.948 --> 01:37:38.240 line:84%
And your argument is that
no, look at all this richness

01:37:38.240 --> 01:37:39.230 line:90%
in the language.

01:37:39.230 --> 01:37:43.580 line:84%
So I want to stress that,
because often we still find

01:37:43.580 --> 01:37:45.885 line:84%
in various circles the
notion that Creoles

01:37:45.885 --> 01:37:46.760 line:90%
evolved from Pidgins.

01:37:46.760 --> 01:37:50.510 line:84%
And yet you've given us
through history yet another set

01:37:50.510 --> 01:37:52.850 line:90%
of arguments against that view.

01:37:52.850 --> 01:37:55.840 line:84%
And one thing I wanted
to ask you as well is--

01:37:55.840 --> 01:38:00.850 line:84%
so in your introduction
to your lecture on Monday,

01:38:00.850 --> 01:38:05.890 line:84%
you were introduced as a
historical sociologist, right?

01:38:05.890 --> 01:38:09.040 line:84%
But when I speak to you, I often
speak to you as a linguist,

01:38:09.040 --> 01:38:09.730 line:90%
you see?

01:38:09.730 --> 01:38:16.240 line:84%
What you tell me about Kreyòl,
about our national language,

01:38:16.240 --> 01:38:19.870 line:84%
you share all this data that
I as a linguist can so deeply

01:38:19.870 --> 01:38:21.880 line:90%
appreciate, you see?

01:38:21.880 --> 01:38:24.070 line:84%
So I want to ask
you, so why are you

01:38:24.070 --> 01:38:27.340 line:84%
being called a historical
sociologist when

01:38:27.340 --> 01:38:31.120 line:84%
in fact, so much of
your work is linguistic?

01:38:31.120 --> 01:38:33.827 line:84%
And here actually I'm playing
devil's advocate, right?

01:38:33.827 --> 01:38:36.160 line:84%
I don't want to say that you
should be one or the other.

01:38:36.160 --> 01:38:40.870 line:84%
I just I just want to hear
you talk about the role

01:38:40.870 --> 01:38:44.500 line:84%
that linguistics and language
make it even more explicit how

01:38:44.500 --> 01:38:48.370 line:84%
it plays into your work as a
sociologist and as a historian,

01:38:48.370 --> 01:38:49.210 line:90%
because I love it.

01:38:49.210 --> 01:38:51.700 line:84%
And I think that in
a way you give us

01:38:51.700 --> 01:38:54.850 line:84%
a model of how the human
sciences can really

01:38:54.850 --> 01:38:58.448 line:84%
operate together to discover our
humanity, our common humanity.

01:38:58.448 --> 01:39:00.490 line:84%
So that's-- and so I want
to praise you for that.

01:39:00.490 --> 01:39:03.700 line:84%
But I want you to say
something more about it.

01:39:03.700 --> 01:39:10.010 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Let me
say that this idea come

01:39:10.010 --> 01:39:17.090 line:84%
from the group of friends
with whom I unite every year.

01:39:17.090 --> 01:39:20.810 line:84%
The Maria Lugones
Decolonial School, no?

01:39:20.810 --> 01:39:27.810 line:84%
It's a summer school
we have every year

01:39:27.810 --> 01:39:30.510 line:90%
during three weeks of--

01:39:30.510 --> 01:39:32.790 line:84%
three weeks, no--
in Holland organized

01:39:32.790 --> 01:39:35.640 line:84%
by the University
of Utrecht and Duke

01:39:35.640 --> 01:39:40.560 line:84%
University's Center
with Walter Mignolo.

01:39:40.560 --> 01:39:43.350 line:84%
And they do not
believe in disciplines.

01:39:43.350 --> 01:39:46.470 line:84%
This group is formed by
philosophers, historians,

01:39:46.470 --> 01:39:51.330 line:84%
economists, sociologists,
artists, of performers,

01:39:51.330 --> 01:39:53.910 line:84%
and all sorts of artists
and we are thinking.

01:39:53.910 --> 01:39:57.110 line:90%
And oh, I forget Catherine.

01:39:57.110 --> 01:40:00.900 line:84%
She's a sociologist
and activist.

01:40:00.900 --> 01:40:05.640 line:84%
But anyway, and this
group, no, they do not even

01:40:05.640 --> 01:40:06.990 line:90%
speak of "university."

01:40:06.990 --> 01:40:09.330 line:90%
They speak of "pluriversity."

01:40:09.330 --> 01:40:10.470 line:90%
You know what I mean?

01:40:10.470 --> 01:40:14.250 line:84%
I mean, this is-- we have
to save our plurality.

01:40:14.250 --> 01:40:17.040 line:84%
We have to save our
diversity, because this

01:40:17.040 --> 01:40:20.130 line:84%
is what I was saying,
the "universality"

01:40:20.130 --> 01:40:22.890 line:84%
that French and the
capitalist system want,

01:40:22.890 --> 01:40:26.400 line:84%
because they want one
"universal" civilization,

01:40:26.400 --> 01:40:27.030 line:90%
God knows what.

01:40:27.030 --> 01:40:30.570 line:84%
No, no, no, no, we will probably
have that "universal" thing.

01:40:30.570 --> 01:40:31.950 line:90%
I won't be there to look at it.

01:40:31.950 --> 01:40:35.310 line:84%
But when we all in our
diversity we contribute,

01:40:35.310 --> 01:40:38.220 line:84%
now we are negotiating
maybe a "university."

01:40:38.220 --> 01:40:40.300 line:84%
For the time being,
it's "pluriversity."

01:40:40.300 --> 01:40:44.610 line:84%
So therefore, you
raise the question,

01:40:44.610 --> 01:40:48.270 line:84%
how is Haiti so
quote unquote "poor?"

01:40:48.270 --> 01:40:51.780 line:90%
So I say, what is Haiti?

01:40:51.780 --> 01:40:55.270 line:84%
Well, the Haitians--
what is a Haitian?

01:40:55.270 --> 01:40:56.940 line:84%
For instance, De
Vastey says a Haitian

01:40:56.940 --> 01:40:59.860 line:84%
is anybody living in the country
having no other place to live.

01:40:59.860 --> 01:41:03.570 line:84%
So the Tainos are as Haitian
as all of those are Haitians.

01:41:03.570 --> 01:41:06.180 line:84%
OK, but if they
came from Africa one

01:41:06.180 --> 01:41:09.720 line:84%
by one, how come
they become Haitians

01:41:09.720 --> 01:41:11.970 line:84%
or whatever other
countries you have, no?

01:41:11.970 --> 01:41:15.360 line:84%
And you see what are the
institutions and the process.

01:41:15.360 --> 01:41:17.940 line:84%
And local history
become the heart

01:41:17.940 --> 01:41:20.520 line:84%
of the reflection,
local history.

01:41:20.520 --> 01:41:22.830 line:84%
I mean, by local
history, it's not

01:41:22.830 --> 01:41:25.050 line:84%
the local history you
find in the archives,

01:41:25.050 --> 01:41:27.780 line:84%
because in the archives you will
find the local history of those

01:41:27.780 --> 01:41:29.700 line:90%
who won the war.

01:41:29.700 --> 01:41:34.500 line:84%
So you have to see you people,
who is speaking, because you--

01:41:34.500 --> 01:41:36.120 line:90%
and to whom are you speaking?

01:41:36.120 --> 01:41:37.770 line:90%
You are speaking to your people.

01:41:37.770 --> 01:41:39.810 line:84%
You have to explain
to them what is it

01:41:39.810 --> 01:41:41.470 line:90%
that they have done themselves.

01:41:41.470 --> 01:41:46.320 line:84%
This is why I cannot accept that
the Caribbean will be British

01:41:46.320 --> 01:41:48.870 line:84%
Caribbean, French Caribbean,
Spanish Caribbean.

01:41:48.870 --> 01:41:51.360 line:84%
Come on, who are in the
Caribbean, therefore?

01:41:51.360 --> 01:41:53.340 line:90%
I mean, are we British?

01:41:53.340 --> 01:41:54.270 line:90%
Are we French?

01:41:54.270 --> 01:41:55.980 line:90%
No, no, we are Caribbean.

01:41:55.980 --> 01:41:58.360 line:84%
And we have made certain
number of things.

01:41:58.360 --> 01:42:01.500 line:84%
But not because we are
Caribbean we are all similar.

01:42:01.500 --> 01:42:03.240 line:90%
We have also a variety in that.

01:42:03.240 --> 01:42:06.360 line:84%
And this is for instance,
the real problem

01:42:06.360 --> 01:42:08.010 line:90%
when they say we are Blacks.

01:42:08.010 --> 01:42:08.820 line:90%
We are Blacks.

01:42:08.820 --> 01:42:12.030 line:84%
OK, "Black" is an invention
of the colonial world,

01:42:12.030 --> 01:42:13.500 line:90%
of the modern colonial world.

01:42:13.500 --> 01:42:17.500 line:84%
The modern colonial world
wants all of us to be the same,

01:42:17.500 --> 01:42:19.660 line:90%
so we can do whatever they want.

01:42:19.660 --> 01:42:24.360 line:84%
So for instance, the
richest Blacks among Blacks

01:42:24.360 --> 01:42:26.280 line:90%
will teach the other Blacks.

01:42:26.280 --> 01:42:30.130 line:84%
No, no, we all of
us have our values.

01:42:30.130 --> 01:42:31.440 line:90%
We all respect ourselves.

01:42:31.440 --> 01:42:33.075 line:90%
And we want to listen to you.

01:42:33.075 --> 01:42:35.160 line:90%
We want you to listen to us, no?

01:42:35.160 --> 01:42:37.877 line:84%
And out of this
variety of Blacks,

01:42:37.877 --> 01:42:39.210 line:90%
if you are to call them Blacks--

01:42:39.210 --> 01:42:41.700 line:84%
I don't call them
Black, but that's OK,

01:42:41.700 --> 01:42:42.870 line:90%
et cetera, et cetera.

01:42:42.870 --> 01:42:48.470 line:84%
And I think this is something
you have while you conquering

01:42:48.470 --> 01:42:49.550 line:90%
your sovereignty.

01:42:49.550 --> 01:42:53.060 line:84%
For instance, as far as I'm
concerned, Haiti's 1970--

01:42:53.060 --> 01:42:58.310 line:84%
1791 was able to
join together people

01:42:58.310 --> 01:43:02.780 line:84%
from different nations of
Africa and create Haiti.

01:43:02.780 --> 01:43:10.350 line:84%
But when you reach for
instance 2010, 2011 in the US,

01:43:10.350 --> 01:43:13.260 line:84%
you see all the oppressed
of the US come and say

01:43:13.260 --> 01:43:16.050 line:90%
"Black lives matter."

01:43:16.050 --> 01:43:18.480 line:84%
And when you look at
those who are saying that,

01:43:18.480 --> 01:43:20.580 line:84%
you have to make an effort
to find all the Blacks.

01:43:20.580 --> 01:43:24.060 line:84%
You find all the people
that are the most exploited

01:43:24.060 --> 01:43:24.870 line:90%
is the Black.

01:43:24.870 --> 01:43:26.820 line:84%
But you find all
sorts of Latins,

01:43:26.820 --> 01:43:29.760 line:84%
all sorts of Amerindians,
all sorts of whites

01:43:29.760 --> 01:43:30.930 line:90%
who have been exploited.

01:43:30.930 --> 01:43:33.480 line:84%
And they are "Black
lives matter."

01:43:33.480 --> 01:43:38.580 line:84%
And if this respect for
the life of the oppressed

01:43:38.580 --> 01:43:40.980 line:84%
become effective,
if you can impose

01:43:40.980 --> 01:43:44.550 line:84%
that on the dominant
system, I think many things

01:43:44.550 --> 01:43:45.730 line:90%
are going to change.

01:43:45.730 --> 01:43:47.580 line:84%
In fact, what you
have is the end

01:43:47.580 --> 01:43:53.810 line:84%
of an era, an era that is the
era where life does not matter,

01:43:53.810 --> 01:43:54.680 line:90%
period.

01:43:54.680 --> 01:43:59.870 line:84%
I mean, there is a wonderful
movie that just come out,

01:43:59.870 --> 01:44:00.950 line:90%
"Don't Look Up."

01:44:00.950 --> 01:44:03.380 line:84%
I don't [INAUDIBLE]----
that's a wonderful--

01:44:03.380 --> 01:44:06.620 line:84%
life does not
matter, simply not.

01:44:06.620 --> 01:44:08.310 line:90%
They have to make more money.

01:44:08.310 --> 01:44:09.260 line:90%
It's a nice movie.

01:44:09.260 --> 01:44:12.560 line:90%
It's sad, really.

01:44:12.560 --> 01:44:14.850 line:90%
It's sad, but it's comical.

01:44:14.850 --> 01:44:16.310 line:90%
I mean, it's cynic--

01:44:16.310 --> 01:44:19.527 line:84%
I don't know more than sad,
it's cynical, no more than--

01:44:19.527 --> 01:44:20.360 line:90%
you see what I mean?

01:44:20.360 --> 01:44:21.450 line:90%
This is the point.

01:44:21.450 --> 01:44:23.540 line:84%
We are saying "Black
lives matter."

01:44:23.540 --> 01:44:27.290 line:84%
We are saying life matters,
period, the life of the Earth,

01:44:27.290 --> 01:44:28.430 line:90%
the life of--

01:44:28.430 --> 01:44:30.360 line:90%
every life matters.

01:44:30.360 --> 01:44:32.720 line:84%
And if you can reach
that point, you

01:44:32.720 --> 01:44:38.300 line:84%
stop that system defined as
the producer of inequality.

01:44:38.300 --> 01:44:42.170 line:84%
I mean, this is the point,
because "Black" is simply

01:44:42.170 --> 01:44:43.580 line:90%
an element of inequality.

01:44:43.580 --> 01:44:45.590 line:84%
You can imagine that
fellow made a mistake.

01:44:45.590 --> 01:44:47.690 line:84%
And he called all
those Indigenous people

01:44:47.690 --> 01:44:48.830 line:90%
"all those Indians."

01:44:48.830 --> 01:44:50.750 line:90%
But that's his mistake.

01:44:50.750 --> 01:44:52.730 line:84%
Why should I carry
that mistake today

01:44:52.730 --> 01:44:54.500 line:90%
when I know it's a mistake?

01:44:54.500 --> 01:44:56.090 line:90%
This is worse.

01:44:56.090 --> 01:44:58.160 line:90%
This is the problem.

01:44:58.160 --> 01:45:00.980 line:84%
In Trinidad we call
East Indian etc. etc.

01:45:00.980 --> 01:45:03.290 line:84%
Well, why should
I say East Indian?

01:45:03.290 --> 01:45:06.200 line:84%
And we are supposed to be West
Indians, instead of mistakes,

01:45:06.200 --> 01:45:08.360 line:84%
eh, because West
Indian originally-- you

01:45:08.360 --> 01:45:14.180 line:84%
know it ends, no,
in the Philippines.

01:45:14.180 --> 01:45:17.630 line:84%
Everything that was from
the West was West Indians.

01:45:17.630 --> 01:45:20.570 line:84%
From us, America, way
down to Philippines,

01:45:20.570 --> 01:45:22.010 line:90%
that was the West Indian.

01:45:22.010 --> 01:45:24.380 line:84%
I mean, this mistake I
don't have to carry it

01:45:24.380 --> 01:45:28.160 line:90%
in the 21st century.

01:45:28.160 --> 01:45:31.640 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
OK, so it's 2:57.

01:45:31.640 --> 01:45:33.000 line:90%
So we should think of closing.

01:45:33.000 --> 01:45:36.170 line:84%
I see that Merelin--
so can you make it--

01:45:36.170 --> 01:45:37.668 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: You
have a question?

01:45:37.668 --> 01:45:38.210 line:90%
STUDENT: Yes.

01:45:38.210 --> 01:45:40.220 line:84%
So like this whole
idea, it's like, how

01:45:40.220 --> 01:45:41.853 line:90%
do we have this conversation--

01:45:41.853 --> 01:45:44.270 line:84%
how do we have this conversation
outside of the classroom?

01:45:44.270 --> 01:45:46.650 line:84%
And if Black and race
is a social construct--

01:45:46.650 --> 01:45:47.900 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: I don't get you.

01:45:47.900 --> 01:45:48.410 line:90%
Come again.

01:45:48.410 --> 01:45:51.110 line:84%
STUDENT: Oh, like how do we have
these types of conversations

01:45:51.110 --> 01:45:53.670 line:84%
outside of the classroom,
because we are having it.

01:45:53.670 --> 01:45:55.790 line:84%
And it's a difficult
conversation.

01:45:55.790 --> 01:45:59.038 line:84%
And also, like if "Black" and
"race" are a social construct,

01:45:59.038 --> 01:46:00.830 line:84%
then what are we supposed
to identify with,

01:46:00.830 --> 01:46:03.830 line:84%
especially like in a
country where Americans, we

01:46:03.830 --> 01:46:05.220 line:90%
like to categorize things?

01:46:05.220 --> 01:46:06.700 line:90%
So it's like, what do we do?

01:46:06.700 --> 01:46:08.360 line:90%
Like for me, I struggle.

01:46:08.360 --> 01:46:10.460 line:84%
I struggle a lot
to say what I am.

01:46:10.460 --> 01:46:11.480 line:90%
Like, I am Dominican.

01:46:11.480 --> 01:46:12.380 line:90%
I am Caribbean.

01:46:12.380 --> 01:46:15.410 line:84%
But like in the US, they
do focus a lot on race.

01:46:15.410 --> 01:46:17.600 line:84%
So it's like, what
are we supposed to--

01:46:17.600 --> 01:46:19.250 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No,
I cannot tell you.

01:46:19.250 --> 01:46:23.120 line:84%
What I can tell you is that
it's a matter of local history.

01:46:23.120 --> 01:46:26.570 line:84%
In Haiti, we end up
creating the Haitians.

01:46:26.570 --> 01:46:29.360 line:84%
In Jamaica, we end up
creating the Jamaicans.

01:46:29.360 --> 01:46:32.600 line:84%
In Haiti or in Jamaica or in
any country of the Caribbean,

01:46:32.600 --> 01:46:36.230 line:90%
Blacks are the majority, no?

01:46:36.230 --> 01:46:38.510 line:84%
Then I will tell you about
the Dominican [INAUDIBLE]

01:46:38.510 --> 01:46:40.370 line:84%
is something that
I am the only one

01:46:40.370 --> 01:46:42.980 line:84%
to accept, not the
Dominicans, not the Haitians.

01:46:42.980 --> 01:46:44.480 line:90%
Make me remember that.

01:46:44.480 --> 01:46:48.170 line:90%
The problem is local history.

01:46:48.170 --> 01:46:53.300 line:84%
Blacks in the US
are 1 out of 10.

01:46:53.300 --> 01:46:58.590 line:84%
In Haiti we are 99 out of 100
or in most Caribbean countries.

01:46:58.590 --> 01:46:59.780 line:90%
So Black is what exists.

01:46:59.780 --> 01:47:01.580 line:90%
So you cannot say--

01:47:01.580 --> 01:47:03.647 line:84%
one day I told a friend
in Haiti, you say,

01:47:03.647 --> 01:47:06.230 line:84%
"Black is beautiful," you know
what they're going to tell you?

01:47:06.230 --> 01:47:07.550 line:90%
"Is there anything else?"

01:47:07.550 --> 01:47:10.070 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:47:10.070 --> 01:47:11.480 line:90%
Because we are all Black.

01:47:11.480 --> 01:47:13.040 line:84%
How do I need not
to be beautiful?

01:47:13.040 --> 01:47:15.310 line:90%
[APPLAUSE] [LAUGHTER]

01:47:17.427 --> 01:47:19.760 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: It's to-- end
of that term you have to--

01:47:19.760 --> 01:47:21.135 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: You
have to finish?

01:47:21.135 --> 01:47:23.010 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: --has
to close, right, Tom?

01:47:23.010 --> 01:47:23.510 line:90%
Yes.

01:47:27.550 --> 01:47:28.240 line:90%
So--

01:47:28.240 --> 01:47:30.100 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No, I
haven't finished me.

01:47:30.100 --> 01:47:31.540 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:47:31.540 --> 01:47:32.040 line:90%
But

01:47:32.040 --> 01:47:33.240 line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:47:33.240 --> 01:47:34.573 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: OK, so, so good.

01:47:34.573 --> 01:47:36.630 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: Let
me try to do me.

01:47:36.630 --> 01:47:38.600 line:84%
So I think this is
where the local history.

01:47:38.600 --> 01:47:43.760 line:84%
I can understand the US
African-American ending up

01:47:43.760 --> 01:47:46.310 line:84%
identifying himself as
"Black," because this

01:47:46.310 --> 01:47:51.200 line:84%
is what unites them, that
held their local history.

01:47:51.200 --> 01:47:54.380 line:90%
That is not our local history.

01:47:54.380 --> 01:47:56.430 line:84%
And I can tell you,
I suffer for that.

01:47:56.430 --> 01:48:00.170 line:84%
But because I went one day and
say something about Blacks.

01:48:00.170 --> 01:48:04.460 line:84%
I say, well, in Haiti,
Blacks, that's not an issue.

01:48:04.460 --> 01:48:06.440 line:90%
Oh boy, imagine.

01:48:06.440 --> 01:48:08.120 line:90%
OK, that's one thing.

01:48:08.120 --> 01:48:10.220 line:84%
So the problem is
to see how "Black"

01:48:10.220 --> 01:48:12.380 line:90%
appears in a given context.

01:48:12.380 --> 01:48:17.450 line:84%
Now, for my Dominican
Republic problems--

01:48:17.450 --> 01:48:19.790 line:84%
the Dominican Republic say
"Black-- that's in Haiti.

01:48:19.790 --> 01:48:20.780 line:90%
I'm not Black.

01:48:20.780 --> 01:48:24.260 line:90%
I'm a dark Indian, not a Black."

01:48:24.260 --> 01:48:27.920 line:84%
Obviously this is
scandalous for those

01:48:27.920 --> 01:48:31.310 line:84%
who are not Dominican Republic,
except Jean Casimir, because I

01:48:31.310 --> 01:48:35.840 line:84%
say, listen, go to Dominica,
the island of Dominica,

01:48:35.840 --> 01:48:41.240 line:84%
an island which has received a
large part of land as a reserve

01:48:41.240 --> 01:48:46.520 line:84%
where the Amerindians
or the Dominicans lives

01:48:46.520 --> 01:48:48.170 line:90%
and they reproduce themselves.

01:48:48.170 --> 01:48:56.600 line:84%
In their society since colonial
time, men who make a kid with--

01:48:56.600 --> 01:48:57.770 line:90%
I mean, they are Indians.

01:48:57.770 --> 01:48:59.720 line:84%
They are-- ah,
not Tainos, Carib.

01:48:59.720 --> 01:49:09.550 line:84%
Men who have an offspring
with a Black woman, the kid

01:49:09.550 --> 01:49:12.910 line:90%
is Amerindian he's carrying.

01:49:12.910 --> 01:49:19.040 line:84%
Women of the Caribs who make
a kid with a Black outside,

01:49:19.040 --> 01:49:21.510 line:90%
the kid is Black.

01:49:21.510 --> 01:49:25.890 line:84%
So you can imagine you cannot
distinguish between Black and--

01:49:25.890 --> 01:49:29.070 line:84%
because if you living in
the Carib among the Caribs,

01:49:29.070 --> 01:49:32.700 line:84%
you're a Carib, and
et cetera, et cetera.

01:49:32.700 --> 01:49:36.060 line:84%
If you are in Dominican
Republic, you are Ladino,

01:49:36.060 --> 01:49:39.600 line:84%
or you are a slave bought
by Christopher Columbus,

01:49:39.600 --> 01:49:42.600 line:84%
and you get in
marronage, you go and you

01:49:42.600 --> 01:49:46.116 line:90%
are received in an Indian--

01:49:46.116 --> 01:49:51.930 line:84%
an Indian, ha, Taino
community, you have a kid,

01:49:51.930 --> 01:49:54.060 line:90%
the kid is going to be Black.

01:49:54.060 --> 01:49:55.710 line:90%
He's to be Taino, period.

01:49:55.710 --> 01:49:58.630 line:84%
He's not Black, no
matter how the color is,

01:49:58.630 --> 01:49:59.830 line:90%
and so on and so forth.

01:49:59.830 --> 01:50:04.140 line:84%
Now if you are receiving
Blacks in Haiti

01:50:04.140 --> 01:50:06.240 line:84%
and they will come
in the DR, they

01:50:06.240 --> 01:50:11.980 line:84%
are Blacks, because they are
not born in an Indian society.

01:50:11.980 --> 01:50:12.890 line:90%
You see what I mean?

01:50:12.890 --> 01:50:14.665 line:84%
So I find normal
that the Indians

01:50:14.665 --> 01:50:15.790 line:90%
say the Haitians are Black.

01:50:15.790 --> 01:50:18.280 line:90%
They are not Black.

01:50:18.280 --> 01:50:21.640 line:84%
They are Indians, dark
Indian, but they are Indians,

01:50:21.640 --> 01:50:23.620 line:90%
because this is the population.

01:50:23.620 --> 01:50:26.360 line:84%
Now Christopher-- not
Christopher Columbus.

01:50:26.360 --> 01:50:28.480 line:90%
Ha, yeah, that's a good slip.

01:50:28.480 --> 01:50:32.200 line:84%
Rafael Leonidas Trujillo--
now we'll use [LAUGHS] that

01:50:32.200 --> 01:50:37.090 line:84%
as the slip-- will use that
to make of the Black something

01:50:37.090 --> 01:50:40.480 line:84%
different and to discriminate
against the Haitians and also

01:50:40.480 --> 01:50:42.400 line:84%
to discriminate
against the Black--

01:50:42.400 --> 01:50:42.790 line:90%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: Dominicans.

01:50:42.790 --> 01:50:45.165 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: --Dominicans,
because et cetera, et cetera.

01:50:45.165 --> 01:50:48.250 line:84%
But I am not supposed to
carry the sin of Chris--

01:50:48.250 --> 01:50:50.710 line:84%
Mr. Rafael Leonidas
Trujillo, the same way

01:50:50.710 --> 01:50:56.500 line:84%
I don't want to carry
the sin of Columbus, no?

01:50:56.500 --> 01:51:00.370 line:84%
So I have to acknowledge the
normal Dominican is right.

01:51:00.370 --> 01:51:02.350 line:90%
"Black" is Haitian.

01:51:02.350 --> 01:51:03.790 line:90%
He is not that... well--

01:51:03.790 --> 01:51:05.170 line:90%
But nobody accept that.

01:51:05.170 --> 01:51:05.770 line:90%
[LAUGHS]

01:51:05.770 --> 01:51:07.270 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: But
didn't you say--

01:51:07.270 --> 01:51:09.400 line:84%
was that you also
may have mentioned,

01:51:09.400 --> 01:51:12.460 line:84%
but it's a whole discussion that
there's no time to get into,

01:51:12.460 --> 01:51:16.120 line:84%
that in Haiti, there
is colorism, right?

01:51:16.120 --> 01:51:17.560 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Obviously.

01:51:17.560 --> 01:51:18.490 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: That's
mixed with class.

01:51:18.490 --> 01:51:20.615 line:84%
So there's that famous
saying of Jean-Jacques Accau

01:51:20.615 --> 01:51:24.790 line:84%
that a rich black is a
mulatto and a poor mulatto

01:51:24.790 --> 01:51:25.465 line:90%
is black, right?

01:51:25.465 --> 01:51:28.090 line:84%
JEAN CASIMIR: No, no, providing
he knows how to read and write.

01:51:28.090 --> 01:51:29.980 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF:
Exactly, exactly,

01:51:29.980 --> 01:51:31.060 line:90%
so anyway, so that's a--

01:51:31.060 --> 01:51:32.530 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: Whole other story.

01:51:32.530 --> 01:51:34.238 line:84%
MICHEL DEGRAFF: We
have no time for that.

01:51:34.238 --> 01:51:37.180 line:84%
So let's be extremely,
extremely thankful

01:51:37.180 --> 01:51:39.640 line:84%
and I'd like to give a
big round of applause

01:51:39.640 --> 01:51:40.840 line:90%
to Professor Jean Casimir.

01:51:40.840 --> 01:51:41.050 line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

01:51:41.050 --> 01:51:41.670 line:90%
JEAN CASIMIR: That was nice.

01:51:41.670 --> 01:51:42.290 line:90%
That was nice.

01:51:42.290 --> 01:51:44.950 line:84%
I applaud to you
for your questions.

01:51:44.950 --> 01:51:47.650 line:90%
It was nice to meet you.

01:51:47.650 --> 01:51:49.500 line:90%
Thank you very much.

